/* Roofline part callouts.
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Tinted polygons were the wrong instinct: they read as highlighter pen and a
   few pixels of placement error is glaring on a shape. A marker needs ONE
   coordinate, a ring plus a hairline plus a small label, and the same error is
   invisible. It is also the register this page was asked for â€” the part is
   pointed AT rather than painted over.

   THE BOX REPRODUCES THE CANVAS COVER TRANSFORM so a percentage means what it
   meant on the coordinate grid laid over the source photograph, which is
   1920x1280. Replace that photograph and this aspect ratio changes with it or
   every marker slides.

   `:has()` reads the `is-active` class the story controller already sets, so
   none of this needs JavaScript. Panel 1 is the H1, parts start at panel 2. */
.fg-rl-parts {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  aspect-ratio: 1920 / 1280;
  min-width: 100%;
  min-height: 100%;
  z-index: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
}

.fg-aw-story--roofline .fg-aw-story__content {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 2;
}

.fg-rl-parts__scrim {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  opacity: 0;
  background: rgba(2, 10, 9, 0.88);
  transition: opacity 600ms ease;
}

.fg-rl-pin {
  position: absolute;
  left: var(--x);
  top: var(--y);
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.75rem;
  opacity: 0;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(0.96);
  transition: opacity 520ms ease, transform 520ms ease;
}

/* The ring: a hairline circle with a lit core, drawn at a size that reads as an
   instrument marker rather than a map pin. */
.fg-rl-pin i {
  position: relative;
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 15px;
  height: 15px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 6px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07), 0 2px 14px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.45);
}

.fg-rl-pin i::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  inset: 4px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: #ffffff;
}

/* The label sits on a hairline that leaves the ring, so it reads as a callout
   rather than a floating caption. */
.fg-rl-pin b {
  position: relative;
  padding-left: 2.6rem;
  color: #ffffff;
  font-size: 0.82rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  white-space: nowrap;
  text-shadow: 0 1px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6);
}

.fg-rl-pin b::before {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  top: 50%;
  width: 2.1rem;
  height: 1px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.75);
}


/* THE COMPONENT ITSELF LIGHTS, NOT JUST THE MARKER. Owner, 2026-08-15: the whole
   image goes darker apart from the part being talked about. The marker names it,
   the zone proves it, and the two arrive together.

   ZONES COME FROM THE OWNER'S OWN MARKED-UP SCREENS, 2026-08-15, after four
   passes of me estimating them failed. He painted out everything except one
   component on each of two screenshots, which settled exactly where each part
   begins and ends; those boundaries were converted from stage space into this
   box's space. THE FIRST CONVERSION CAME OUT MIRRORED: every zone ascended left
   to right while every component in the photograph descends left to right, which
   is why four rounds of nudging never converged â€” the shapes were right and the
   sign was wrong. Check the slope direction against the picture before adjusting
   any number here.

   ORDER MATTERS AND IT IS THE THING THAT WAS WRONG: gutter sits highest, the
   fascia is the board DIRECTLY BETWEEN the gutter and the soffit, and the soffit
   is the plane below it. Every zone was one band too high, so what was labelled
   fascia was lighting the gutter. Owner correction, 2026-08-15. The soffit's top
   edge is a straight line along the underside of the fascia rather than a slope
   that cuts up through it.

   THE THREE BANDS TOUCH, so they are built from SHARED BOUNDARY LINES rather
   than as three independent shapes. Owner correction, 2026-08-15: there are no
   gaps between a gutter, the fascia under it and the soffit under that, and
   drawing them separately guarantees slivers of dark between them and a soffit
   that leans differently from the board above it.

   THE BOUNDARIES ARE DETECTED, NOT DRAWN OR READ. Nine passes of me estimating
   angles off renders and off the owner's marked-up photo all missed, and the
   reason the marked-up photo could not rescue it is that it is a phone picture
   of a laptop screen: the screen is keystoned, so a straight line drawn on the
   glass is not straight in page coordinates, and the error grows toward the
   edges where these very boundaries sit.

   So the lines come from the photograph itself. A Hough-style sweep over slope
   and intercept scores every candidate line by the mean bright-above/dark-below
   luminance step along its whole length, and returns the two dominant edges:
   the top of the gutter at (0%, -34%) to (100%, 43%), and the fascia-to-soffit
   divide at (0%, 24%) to (100%, 76%). The gutter's underside sits parallel below
   its top edge. **Re-run that detection if the photograph is ever replaced;
   do not adjust these by eye.**

   THEY ARE NOT PARALLEL, and the detection proves it rather than assuming it:
   0.767 along the roof edge against 0.519 at the soffit divide. The two converge
   to the right, so the fascia reads deeper at the left of the frame. Forcing a
   common angle across them, which every earlier attempt did, cannot fit.

   Image percentages ARE clip-path percentages here, because the overlay box is
   given the photograph's own aspect ratio. No conversion, which is one fewer
   place to go wrong.

   Three lines taken off a 10% grid laid over the source photograph
   (which shares this box's aspect ratio, so readings need no conversion): the
   roofline falls 0.63 vertically per unit horizontally, measured along the
   gutter run rather than guessed. Line A is the top of the
   gutter, B the gutter-to-fascia junction, C the fascia-to-soffit junction. The
   gutter is A to B, the fascia is B to C, the soffit is C to the bottom of the
   frame. Change one boundary and the band on each side of it follows, which is
   the point of writing them this way.

   Values run past 0% because the roofline exits the top of the frame on the
   left; clip-path handles that and it keeps the maths honest rather than
   clamping and bending the line.

   THE FASCIA RUNS THE FULL WIDTH OF THE FRAME. It was starting at 50% and
   lighting only the right-hand half of a board that carries on to the left edge,
   rising as it goes because the whole roofline descends left to right. Carried
   out to x=0 on the same slope; anything past the top of the frame simply
   clips. **If the stage height or the source photograph changes,
   re-derive rather than nudge.** They are
   generous rather than tight: a soft, slightly-larger region reads as "this
   area" and survives a few pixels of error, where a tight outline that misses
   by the same margin reads as broken. */
/* THE ZONE REPAINTS THE SOURCE PHOTOGRAPH RATHER THAN BRIGHTENING THE SCRIM.
   Owner, 2026-08-15: the lit part should be at full brightness as in the
   original, and it was reading dull.

   It was `backdrop-filter: brightness(1.9)`, which cannot work over a scrim of
   `rgba(2,10,9,0.88)`: the backdrop is already at 12% of the original, so 1.9x
   only reaches 23%. Getting back to the source needs about 8.3x, and that blows
   the highlights and drains the colour instead of restoring it. **A filter
   cannot undo a scrim** â€” repaint the pixels.

   `--fg-rl-frame` is set on `.fg-rl-parts` in the template and is the same URL
   the canvas draws, so the browser has it cached and this costs no request.
   `background-size: 100% 100%` against a box that is `inset: 0` of that
   container puts it in the same coordinate space as the clip-path percentages
   by construction, with no conversion step to get wrong. Both frames and the
   container are 3:2, so nothing stretches. */
.fg-rl-zone {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 620ms ease;
  background-image: var(--fg-rl-frame);
  background-position: center;
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
  background-size: 100% 100%;
}

/* The canvas swaps to the smaller frame here, so the zone follows it rather
   than pulling the desktop one down a phone connection for a cached hit that
   would then miss. */
@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .fg-rl-zone {
    background-image: var(--fg-rl-frame-mobile, var(--fg-rl-frame));
  }
}

.fg-rl-zone--fascia { clip-path: polygon(0% -18%, 100% 60%, 100% 76%, 0% 24%); }
/* THE SOFFIT'S LOWER EDGE IS DETECTED, NOT THE BOTTOM OF THE FRAME. Owner,
   2026-08-15: the zone was too big. It ran from the fascia divide straight down
   to 100%, so it lit the bifold doors, the glazing, the brick pier and the
   wagon wheel as well as the soffit. That edge had never been measured â€” the
   other three came off the Hough sweep and this one was left at the frame edge,
   which was survivable while the lift was a subtle 1.9x and is not now the zone
   paints the source pixels.

   THE EDGE IS WHERE THE SHIPLAP ENDS AND THE DOOR HEAD BEGINS. Owner, and it is
   the description that finally located it: the boards butt into the head at a
   right angle, so the end is a straight line.

   IT TOOK THREE WRONG ANSWERS AND THE FAULT WAS THE SAME EACH TIME â€” the thing
   being measured, never the measuring.

   1. A luminance sweep scoring bright-above/dark-below returned the GLAZING
      REFLECTION at 61%..98%, scoring 33.3 against 9.2. Real edge, wrong one.
   2. A colour mask of the soffit gave 67.5%..88.0%. But the door head is the
      SAME PAINT, so the mask ran straight through it.
   3. A contiguous-run check was added specifically to catch that, and agreed to
      within 7px â€” because there is no colour break between soffit and head for
      it to stop at either. **Two methods agreeing is worth nothing when they
      share a blind spot.** That agreement was reported as evidence. It was not.

   WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG: polarity. The soffit is DARKER than the pale head bar
   beneath it, and every sweep was scoring bright-above/dark-below, so the edge
   was invisible to the search by construction. No amount of re-running finds an
   edge the scoring function is built to ignore. Flip it and the same sweep
   returns it at 98.3 against 23.4 for the runner up.

   Fitted on x=120..980, where the frame is sharp and the head is unobstructed,
   then checked column by column across the full width: **median residual 1.7px**
   over x=150..1140, eleven columns inside 2px. The two outliers are the door
   mullion at x=600 and the wagon wheel from x=1230, where edge strength halves.
   y = 0.2354x + 754.7 on the 1800x1200 frame: 62.9% at x=0, 98.2% at x=100%.

   Extended straight to both edges because the owner's description is that it is
   straight, and the residuals say so too.

   AND THEN UP 8.0pp MORE, TO 53.7% AND 89.0%, WHICH IS THE PUBLISHED VALUE.
   Slope is untouched throughout: 35.3 percentage points across the frame in every
   version of this line.

   THE EDGE FITTED ABOVE WAS THE WRONG FEATURE. Between the shiplap and the door
   head there is a SMOOTH PALE TRIM BAND, roughly 100px deep on the 1800x1200
   frame, and it is neither. Every automated attempt landed on the bottom of that
   band or on the dark reveal beneath it, because those are where the strong
   luminance edges are. The band has no grooves, so it is not soffit, and lighting
   it is what "catching the head" meant.

   SIX DETECTORS WERE RUN AND ALL SIX MISSED IT. Bright-above sweep found the
   glazing. Colour mask ran through the head, being the same paint. Contiguity
   check shared that blind spot and "confirmed" it. Polarity-flipped sweep found
   the trim's lower edge. Groove-energy segmentation returned a horizontal line.
   Groove-run fitting found nine columns and extrapolated off the frame. What
   settled it was drawing candidate lines at fixed offsets over the photograph and
   looking: the shiplap's end is unmistakable once the alternatives are beside it.
   **If this needs re-deriving, draw the options and look. Do not trust a score.**

   Checked against the luminance profiles afterwards: at x=450 the line lands at
   y=750, immediately past the last groove at 718 and on the bright trim edge at
   754. At x=750 and x=1050 it sits a board or two inside the boarding, which is
   the safe direction â€” missing soffit is invisible, lit trim is not.

   NOT PARALLEL to the edge above it, and that is the geometry rather than an
   error: the divide falls 0.52 across the frame and this falls 0.205, because
   the soffit recedes and its apparent depth shrinks from 43 percentage points
   at the left to 12 at the right. Re-run the detection if the photograph
   changes; do not nudge these by hand. */
.fg-rl-zone--soffit { clip-path: polygon(0% 24%, 100% 76%, 100% 89%, 0% 53.7%); }
/* THE GUTTER'S TOP EDGE IS THE TOP OF ITS BRIGHT FACE, not the bottom of it.
   Owner, 2026-08-15: correct angle, raise it to the next straight line above.
   It was cutting across the gutter and leaving the upper half of the profile in
   the scrim, so the lit band was part of the gutter rather than the gutter.

   Measured the same way the soffit finally was â€” read the luminance profile and
   look â€” rather than by another search. The gutter face reads as a bright band
   about 36px deep on the 1800x1200 frame; the old edge sat on its lower
   boundary, so the fix is the upper one. Walking to the top of that band across
   46 columns and fitting gives a span of 79.8 percentage points against the
   77.0 the line already had, which is the owner's "correct angle" confirmed
   independently: within 2.8pp over the whole frame.

   So the angle is held exactly and only the height changes, by the median raise
   of 38.4px = 3.2pp. Bottom edge untouched. */
.fg-rl-zone--gutter { clip-path: polygon(0% -37.2%, 100% 39.8%, 100% 60%, 0% -18%); }

.fg-aw-story--roofline:has(.fg-aw-story__panel:nth-child(2).is-active) .fg-rl-parts__scrim,
.fg-aw-story--roofline:has(.fg-aw-story__panel:nth-child(3).is-active) .fg-rl-parts__scrim,
.fg-aw-story--roofline:has(.fg-aw-story__panel:nth-child(4).is-active) .fg-rl-parts__scrim,
.fg-aw-story--roofline:has(.fg-aw-story__panel:nth-child(4).is-active) .fg-rl-parts__scrim {
  opacity: 1;
}

.fg-aw-story--roofline:has(.fg-aw-story__panel:nth-child(2).is-active) .fg-rl-pin--fascia,
.fg-aw-story--roofline:has(.fg-aw-story__panel:nth-child(3).is-active) .fg-rl-pin--soffit,
.fg-aw-story--roofline:has(.fg-aw-story__panel:nth-child(4).is-active) .fg-rl-pin--gutter {
  opacity: 1;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1);
}

/* Three parts, three panels. Ventilation was dropped as a point entirely on the
   owner's instruction: this photograph shows no vent, and a marker pointing at
   where one would go is the kind of near-miss that reads as a mistake. */
.fg-aw-story--roofline:has(.fg-aw-story__panel:nth-child(2).is-active) .fg-rl-zone--fascia,
.fg-aw-story--roofline:has(.fg-aw-story__panel:nth-child(3).is-active) .fg-rl-zone--soffit,
.fg-aw-story--roofline:has(.fg-aw-story__panel:nth-child(4).is-active) .fg-rl-zone--gutter {
  opacity: 1;
}

/* Below the phone breakpoint the copy fills the stage, so the labels would sit
   under it. The rings stay, the words go. */
@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .fg-rl-pin b { display: none; }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .fg-rl-parts__scrim,
  .fg-rl-pin,
  .fg-rl-zone { transition: none; }
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Slide and fold doors (/slide-fold-doors/), rebuilt 2026-08-18.

   Almost nothing here is new. The hero is `.fg-cd3-hero`, joined to that
   component's selector lists above rather than copied, so restyling composite
   doors restyles this too. The middle is the shared `.fg-cw` split grammar the
   heritage door, flush casement and aluminium door pages already use. What is
   left below is the two things this route genuinely owns: a video sitting in a
   box built for an image, and the span and material blocks.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* The film sits in the shared full-bleed product hero, so it inherits
   `.fg-hero__video`'s cover sizing and needs no geometry of its own.

   What it owns is the pause control. The native `controls` bar was tried and
   pulled: it paints black player chrome across the poster from first paint,
   which is unacceptable on a hero. A looping film still needs a pause, so this
   is that, in the site's own language. Bottom right, diagonally opposite the
   hero copy, which sits bottom left. */
.fg-sf-hero__toggle {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 2;
  right: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2rem);
  bottom: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2rem);
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 44px;
  height: 44px;
  padding: 0;
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55);
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: rgba(6, 33, 42, 0.55);
  color: #fff;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background 200ms ease, border-color 200ms ease, transform 200ms ease;
}

/* 44px is the tap-target floor and the button is exactly on it, so it does not
   need a hit-area pad the way the footer consent buttons did. */
.fg-sf-hero__toggle:hover,
.fg-sf-hero__toggle:focus-visible {
  border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
  background: rgba(6, 33, 42, 0.78);
}

/* A CSS triangle for play, two bars for pause, so there is no icon font and no
   SVG to ship. The state comes from `is-playing`, which the controller sets off
   the video's real play/pause events rather than off the click. */
.fg-sf-hero__toggle-icon {
  display: block;
  width: 0;
  height: 0;
  margin-left: 3px;
  border-top: 7px solid transparent;
  border-bottom: 7px solid transparent;
  border-left: 11px solid currentColor;
}

.fg-sf-hero__toggle.is-playing .fg-sf-hero__toggle-icon {
  width: 11px;
  height: 13px;
  margin-left: 0;
  border: 0;
  border-left: 4px solid currentColor;
  border-right: 4px solid currentColor;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .fg-sf-hero__toggle {
    transition: none;
  }
}

/* THE SPAN BAND: the page's one change of register.

   Four alternating light splits read as competent and flat, which is what the
   owner called out on 2026-08-18. The routes that work change shape once in the
   middle, and this is where: scale is the thing a full-bleed photograph can say
   and a boxed one cannot.

   Full-bleed, so it deliberately escapes the page rhythm. The photograph is
   landscape and a different property from the rest of the page. */
.fg-sf-span {
  position: relative;
  isolation: isolate;
  margin: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem) 0;
  padding: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 6rem) 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  background: var(--color-steel);
}

.fg-sf-span__bg {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: -2;
  inset: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
  object-position: 50% 45%;
}

/* The scrim is what makes white type legible over a photograph, and it is a
   gradient rather than a flat wash so the right-hand side of the run stays
   readable as a picture. STYLE.md allows white text only inside a dark panel or
   over an image with a TESTED overlay; this one was measured at 1440 and 390. */
.fg-sf-span__shade {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: -1;
  inset: 0;
  background: linear-gradient(100deg, rgba(6, 33, 42, 0.94) 0%, rgba(6, 33, 42, 0.86) 42%, rgba(6, 33, 42, 0.55) 100%);
}

.fg-sf-span__inner {
  display: grid;
  gap: clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 3rem);
}

.fg-sf-span__copy {
  max-width: 46ch;
}

.fg-sf-span .eyebrow {
  color: #7fd4a3;
}

.fg-sf-span h2,
.fg-sf-span__copy p {
  color: #fff;
}

.fg-sf-span__copy p {
  margin-top: 0.9rem;
}

/* The derived span row. Deliberately not `.fg-cw-tiles`: these are four short
   number-and-label pairs that should read as one row of figures, not as four
   cards. Wraps to two columns before it wraps to one. */
.fg-sf-spans {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: var(--fg-space-4);
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.fg-sf-spans li {
  display: grid;
  gap: 0.15rem;
  padding: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 0.85rem;
  border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
}

.fg-sf-spans strong {
  color: #fff;
  font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.6vw, 2rem);
  line-height: 1.1;
}

.fg-sf-spans span {
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-xs);
}

/* The install shots are portrait 3:4 from a phone. `.fg-cw-media--4x5` is the
   closest box the shared grammar offers, so the crop is small. The caption chip
   names the job, because these are ours and the film in the hero is not an
   installation. */
.fg-sf-shot figcaption {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 1;
  bottom: 1rem;
  left: 1rem;
  padding: 0.5rem 0.65rem;
  border-radius: 4px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
  color: var(--color-ink);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-xs);
  font-weight: 700;
}

.fg-sf-shot {
  position: relative;
}

/* The colour chip.

   The section reuses `.fg-alu-colours` wholesale, so the card, the grid and the
   name/reference typography all come from there and nothing is redeclared. The
   only thing this route owns is the chip that stands in for a photographed
   swatch, sized to the same box the corner renders occupy so the two treatments
   can be swapped without the grid moving.

   THE HAIRLINE IS ON EVERY CHIP, NOT JUST THE PALE ONES. Signal White on a white
   card reads as an empty slot without it, which is the reason the smooth white
   uPVC swatch already carries one, and putting the border on only the light
   chips would single them out as a different kind of thing. */
.fg-sf-colours__chip {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
  border: 1px solid rgba(15, 49, 54, 0.18);
  border-radius: 6px;
  background: var(--chip, var(--color-muted));
}

/* The two stock colours are marked rather than reordered, so the row still
   reads in the manufacturer's own order. */
.fg-sf-colours__grid .is-standard::after {
  content: 'Standard';
  position: absolute;
  top: 0.5rem;
  right: 0.5rem;
  padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem;
  border-radius: 3px;
  background: rgba(6, 33, 42, 0.82);
  color: #fff;
  font-size: 0.62rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}

/* The colour prose sits in the heading's right column, and this alignment was
   got wrong twice before it was got right, so both attempts are recorded.

   The shared component uses `align-items: end`, which suits it: one paragraph
   against a heading of similar height, bottoms aligned. Here the right column is
   the taller, and `end` sinks the H2 to the foot of the prose while the eyebrow
   stays pinned to the top of the grid, leaving a hole between the eyebrow and
   its own heading. `start` puts that space UNDER the heading, where it reads as
   deliberate.

   The other half of the fix was editorial: the prose is ONE paragraph rather
   than two, so the two columns are close enough in height that the gap barely
   shows. If it ever grows back to two, this override stops earning its keep. */
.fg-sf-colours__heading {
  align-items: start;
}

/* Six swatches, not twelve, so the grid is one row. The shared six-column rule
   would leave it correct on desktop and cramped between 861px and 1080px, where
   six cells share a narrower container. Four then two reads better there. */
@media (max-width: 1080px) and (min-width: 861px) {
  .fg-sf-colours__grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }
}

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  /* Two by two rather than four across. At 390px four columns puts "6.0m" and
     "6 panels" into 80px cells and the label wraps to three lines, which is the
     114px-cell fault already recorded against the shared colour label tier. */
  .fg-sf-spans {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }

  /* The gradient runs left to right on desktop, which leaves the bottom of a
     tall phone viewport unscrimmed. Vertical on mobile instead. */
  .fg-sf-span__shade {
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(6, 33, 42, 0.92) 0%, rgba(6, 33, 42, 0.88) 60%, rgba(6, 33, 42, 0.8) 100%);
  }
}
/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   CARE AND MAINTENANCE GUIDES  (/care-and-maintenance/)

   Image-led picker, then one guide at a time. Every panel is in the DOM and the
   JavaScript hides the unselected ones, so the no-JS fallback is the whole
   document rather than an empty box.

   THE PAGE CANVAS IS NOT REPAINTED HERE. `body` already paints
   `--fg-page-gradient` with `background-attachment: fixed`; a second copy on a
   route wrapper stacks a scrolling gradient over the fixed one and bands the
   section joins. STYLE.md records that defect against .fg-fensa-page and
   friends and this page deliberately does not repeat it.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.fg-care-hero {
  padding: clamp(2.25rem, 4vw, 3.5rem) 0 clamp(1.5rem, 2.5vw, 2.25rem);
}

.fg-care-hero__grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(0, 0.95fr);
  gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 3rem);
  align-items: start;
}

.fg-care-hero h1 {
  margin: 0.35rem 0 0.9rem;
  font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 3.6vw, var(--fg-font-size-max));
  line-height: 1.05;
}

.fg-care-hero__lead {
  max-width: 56ch;
  margin: 0 0 0.85rem;
  color: var(--color-steel-90);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-lg);
  line-height: 1.55;
}

.fg-care-hero__actions {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: var(--fg-space-4);
  align-items: center;
  margin-top: 1.35rem;
}

/* Green primary, steel secondary: the site's CTA pairing from the header. */
.fg-care-hero__hours {
  margin: 0.7rem 0 0;
  color: var(--color-muted);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-sm);
}

/* The four universal habits, numbered because they are genuinely ordered by how
   often each one turns out to be the answer. */
.fg-care-hero__rules {
  padding: clamp(1.15rem, 2vw, 1.6rem);
  border: 1px solid rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.12);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
  box-shadow: var(--fg-shadow-md);
}

.fg-care-hero__rules h2 {
  margin: 0.3rem 0 1rem;
  font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 2.2vw, 2rem);
  line-height: 1.2;
}

.fg-care-hero__rules ol {
  display: grid;
  gap: 0.85rem;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
  counter-reset: fg-care-rule;
}

.fg-care-hero__rules li {
  position: relative;
  padding-left: 2.35rem;
  counter-increment: fg-care-rule;
}

.fg-care-hero__rules li::before {
  content: counter(fg-care-rule, decimal-leading-zero);
  position: absolute;
  top: 0.05rem;
  left: 0;
  color: var(--color-accent);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-sm);
  font-weight: 850;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
}

.fg-care-hero__rules strong {
  display: block;
  color: var(--color-steel);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-base);
  line-height: 1.35;
}

.fg-care-hero__rules span {
  display: block;
  margin-top: 0.15rem;
  color: var(--color-muted);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-md);
  line-height: 1.45;
}

/* --- Picker ---------------------------------------------------------- */

.fg-care-selector {
  padding: clamp(1.5rem, 2.5vw, 2.25rem) 0 clamp(2rem, 3.5vw, 3rem);
}

.fg-care-selector__head { margin-bottom: clamp(1.15rem, 2vw, 1.6rem); }

.fg-care-selector__head h2 {
  margin: 0.3rem 0 0;
  font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 2.2vw, 2rem);
}

/* Groups stack down the page and each one spreads its tiles across the full
   width. Three columns of stacked square tiles would have made each group a
   1000px tower. */
.fg-care-picker {
  display: grid;
  gap: clamp(1.35rem, 2.4vw, 2rem);
  margin-bottom: clamp(1.5rem, 2.6vw, 2.25rem);
}

.fg-care-picker__group {
  display: grid;
  align-content: start;
  gap: 0.75rem;
}

.fg-care-picker__group-title {
  margin: 0;
  padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  color: var(--color-steel);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-2xs);
  font-weight: 850;
  letter-spacing: 0.07em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}

.fg-care-picker__cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: clamp(0.7rem, 1.3vw, 1rem);
}

/* SQUARE PHOTO TILE, NAME OVER THE IMAGE.
 *
 * EVERY CHILD IS ABSOLUTELY POSITIONED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The first
 * version laid this out as `display: grid` with two columns on the <button>
 * itself, and the compiled CSS was correct while the rendering was not: the
 * photo sat above the label at full width instead of beside it. A <button>
 * wraps its children in an anonymous box, so `grid-template-columns` never sees
 * two children to place. The guide banner further down worked from the first
 * attempt because it positions its image absolutely, which is what this now
 * does too. If you ever need a multi-column button, take the layout off the
 * button and put it on a span inside it.
 */
.fg-care-card {
  position: relative;
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  padding: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
  border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  border-radius: 12px;
  background: var(--color-soft);
  color: #ffffff;
  text-align: left;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: box-shadow 0.22s ease, transform 0.22s ease, border-color 0.22s ease;
}

.fg-care-card:hover {
  transform: translateY(-2px);
  border-color: rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.6);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-soft);
}

.fg-care-card[aria-selected='true'] {
  border-color: var(--color-accent);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.85), var(--fg-shadow-md);
}

.fg-care-card:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

.fg-care-card__media {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  display: block;
  overflow: hidden;
}

.fg-care-card__media img {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
  /* Held slightly in, so the product fills the square rather than sitting in the
     middle of an establishing shot of somebody's house. */
  transform: scale(1.06);
  transition: transform 0.45s ease;
}

.fg-care-card:hover .fg-care-card__media img,
.fg-care-card[aria-selected='true'] .fg-care-card__media img { transform: scale(1.13); }

/* Two stops rather than a flat wash, so the top of the photograph keeps its
   colour and only the part carrying text is darkened. */
.fg-care-card::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(2, 27, 34, 0) 34%, rgba(2, 27, 34, 0.58) 64%, rgba(2, 27, 34, 0.92) 100%);
  transition: opacity 0.22s ease;
}

.fg-care-card__label {
  position: absolute;
  right: 0;
  bottom: 0;
  left: 0;
  z-index: 1;
  display: grid;
  gap: 0.2rem;
  padding: 0.85rem 0.9rem 0.9rem;
}

.fg-care-card__name {
  color: #ffffff;
  font-size: var(--fg-text-lg);
  font-weight: 850;
  line-height: 1.2;
  text-shadow: 0 1px 12px rgba(2, 27, 34, 0.5);
}

.fg-care-card__covers {
  display: -webkit-box;
  overflow: hidden;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-xs);
  line-height: 1.35;
  text-shadow: 0 1px 10px rgba(2, 27, 34, 0.55);
  -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
  -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
}

/* A green flag in the corner of the open one, so the selection reads at a
   glance across a grid of nine photographs. */
.fg-care-card[aria-selected='true'] .fg-care-card__label::before {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  top: -1.9rem;
  left: 0.9rem;
  width: 1.5rem;
  height: 0.22rem;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: var(--color-accent);
}

/* --- One guide ------------------------------------------------------- */

.fg-care-guide {
  overflow: hidden;
  border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  border-radius: 12px;
  background: #ffffff;
  box-shadow: var(--fg-shadow-md);
}

.fg-care-guide:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
  outline-offset: 3px;
}

/* No-JS fallback: panels stack with air between them, each keeping its own
   hero, so the page still reads as nine separate guides. */
.fg-care-guide + .fg-care-guide { margin-top: clamp(1.5rem, 2.6vw, 2.5rem); }

/* A proper banner rather than a thumbnail beside a paragraph. The scrim is a
   two-stop gradient because a flat overlay greys the whole photograph out. */
.fg-care-guide__hero {
  position: relative;
  isolation: isolate;
  display: flex;
  align-items: flex-end;
  min-height: 210px;
  padding: clamp(1.15rem, 2.4vw, 2rem);
}

.fg-care-guide__hero img {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  z-index: -2;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
}

.fg-care-guide__hero::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  z-index: -1;
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(2, 27, 34, 0.12) 0%, rgba(2, 27, 34, 0.62) 58%, rgba(2, 27, 34, 0.88) 100%);
}

.fg-care-guide__hero-copy { color: #ffffff; }

.fg-care-guide__hero h3 {
  margin: 0.15rem 0 0;
  color: #ffffff;
  font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 2.6vw, 2.35rem);
  line-height: 1.1;
}

.fg-care-guide__covers {
  margin: 0;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-sm);
  font-weight: 750;
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
}

/* Opening: the lead sentence, and the kit you want to hand. */
.fg-care-guide__opening {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.35fr) minmax(0, 0.65fr);
  gap: clamp(1.25rem, 2.4vw, 2.25rem);
  align-items: start;
  padding: clamp(1.25rem, 2.4vw, 2rem);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, #f7fbfa 0%, #ffffff 100%);
}

.fg-care-guide__lead {
  max-width: 58ch;
  margin: 0;
  color: var(--color-steel-90);
  font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.5vw, 1.22rem);
  line-height: 1.6;
}

.fg-care-kit__title {
  margin: 0 0 0.55rem;
  color: var(--color-steel);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-2xs);
  font-weight: 850;
  letter-spacing: 0.07em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}

.fg-care-kit ul {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0.4rem;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.fg-care-kit li {
  padding: 0.3rem 0.65rem;
  border: 1px solid rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.35);
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.08);
  color: var(--color-steel);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-xs);
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1.35;
}

.fg-care-section {
  padding: clamp(1.25rem, 2.4vw, 2rem);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line);
}

.fg-care-section__head { margin-bottom: clamp(0.9rem, 1.6vw, 1.25rem); }

.fg-care-section__head h4 {
  margin: 0.2rem 0 0;
  font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 1.9vw, 1.6rem);
  line-height: 1.2;
}

/* Routine: a card each, with the frequency doing the visual work. Three across
   on desktop turns a long list into something you can take in at a glance. */
.fg-care-routine {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 0.9rem;
}

.fg-care-step {
  display: grid;
  align-content: start;
  gap: 0.3rem;
  padding: 1rem 1.05rem 1.1rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: var(--color-soft);
}

.fg-care-step__when {
  justify-self: start;
  padding: 0.2rem 0.55rem;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: var(--color-steel);
  color: #ffffff;
  font-size: 0.66rem;
  font-weight: 850;
  letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}

.fg-care-step h5 {
  margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
  color: var(--color-steel);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-lg);
  font-weight: 800;
  line-height: 1.3;
}

.fg-care-step p {
  margin: 0;
  color: var(--color-muted);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-md);
  line-height: 1.55;
}

/* Fixes: a question, the steps, then the reassuring bit. Two across so a long
   guide does not become a single column of scrolling. */
.fg-care-fixes {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 0.9rem;
}

.fg-care-fix {
  display: grid;
  align-content: start;
  padding: 1.05rem 1.15rem 1.15rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: #ffffff;
  box-shadow: var(--fg-shadow-sm);
}

.fg-care-fix h5 {
  margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
  color: var(--color-steel);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-lg);
  font-weight: 800;
  line-height: 1.3;
}

.fg-care-fix ol {
  display: grid;
  gap: 0.45rem;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
  counter-reset: fg-care-fix-step;
}

.fg-care-fix li {
  position: relative;
  padding-left: 1.85rem;
  color: var(--color-steel-90);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-md);
  line-height: 1.5;
  counter-increment: fg-care-fix-step;
}

.fg-care-fix li::before {
  content: counter(fg-care-fix-step);
  position: absolute;
  top: 0.05rem;
  left: 0;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 1.3rem;
  height: 1.3rem;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.16);
  color: var(--color-accent-dark);
  font-size: 0.7rem;
  font-weight: 850;
}

.fg-care-fix__note {
  margin: 0.85rem 0 0;
  padding: 0.7rem 0.8rem;
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: var(--color-soft);
  color: var(--color-muted);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-md);
  line-height: 1.55;
}

/* Closing band, in steel so it reads as the different kind of statement it is. */
.fg-care-callus {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
  gap: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 2rem);
  align-items: center;
  padding: clamp(1.25rem, 2.4vw, 2rem);
  background: var(--color-steel);
  color: #ffffff;
}

.fg-care-callus .eyebrow { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72); }

.fg-care-callus h4 {
  margin: 0.2rem 0 0.55rem;
  color: #ffffff;
  font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 1.9vw, 1.6rem);
}

.fg-care-callus p {
  max-width: 74ch;
  margin: 0;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-md);
  line-height: 1.6;
}

.fg-care-callus__actions {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0.6rem;
}

.fg-care-callus .button { white-space: nowrap; }

/* --- Closing bands --------------------------------------------------- */

.fg-care-promise { padding: clamp(1.5rem, 2.5vw, 2.25rem) 0; }

.fg-care-promise__inner {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.35fr) minmax(0, 0.65fr);
  gap: clamp(1.35rem, 2.6vw, 2.5rem);
  align-items: start;
  padding: clamp(1.35rem, 2.4vw, 2rem);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
}

.fg-care-promise h2 {
  margin: 0.3rem 0 0.75rem;
  font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 2.2vw, 2rem);
}

.fg-care-promise p {
  max-width: 66ch;
  margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
  color: var(--color-muted);
  line-height: 1.6;
}

.fg-care-promise__contact {
  display: grid;
  gap: 0.35rem;
  align-content: start;
}

.fg-care-promise__contact .button { justify-self: start; }

.fg-care-promise__email {
  color: var(--color-steel);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-lg);
  font-weight: 800;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.fg-care-promise__email:hover { color: var(--color-accent-dark); }

.fg-care-promise__contact p {
  margin-top: 0.35rem;
  font-size: var(--fg-text-sm);
}

.fg-care-enquiry {
  padding: clamp(1.5rem, 2.5vw, 2.25rem) 0 clamp(2.25rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
}

.fg-care-enquiry__grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.85fr) minmax(0, 1.15fr);
  gap: clamp(1.35rem, 2.6vw, 2.5rem);
  align-items: start;
}

.fg-care-enquiry h2 {
  margin: 0.3rem 0 0.75rem;
  font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 2.2vw, 2rem);
}

.fg-care-enquiry__copy p {
  max-width: 58ch;
  margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
  color: var(--color-muted);
  line-height: 1.6;
}

.fg-care-enquiry__reassurance {
  padding-top: 0.7rem;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  color: var(--color-steel);
  font-weight: 700;
}

/* THE FORM MUST SIT ON A DARK PANEL. `.fg-form label` is
   rgba(255,255,255,0.82) site-wide, so the shared enquiry component is only
   legible against one, and every route that embeds it supplies its own
   (.fg-fensa-enquiry__form is #12363b). Shipping without it put white labels on
   the light page canvas and the fields read as blank grey boxes. */
.fg-care-enquiry__form {
  padding: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: #12363b;
}

.fg-care-enquiry__form .fg-form {
  padding: 0;
  background: transparent;
}

.fg-care-enquiry__form .fg-enquiry-form__compact-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 0.8rem;
}

.fg-care-enquiry__form .fg-enquiry-form__compact-wide { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
.fg-care-enquiry__form .fg-form input { min-height: 48px; }
.fg-care-enquiry__form .fg-form textarea { min-height: 106px; }
.fg-care-enquiry__form .fg-enquiry-form__footer {
  grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
  align-items: center;
}

/* --- Tablet ---------------------------------------------------------- */

@media (max-width: 1080px) {
  .fg-care-picker__cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .fg-care-routine { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .fg-care-fixes { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .fg-care-guide__opening { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
}

/* --- Mobile ---------------------------------------------------------- */

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .fg-care-hero__grid,
  .fg-care-promise__inner,
  .fg-care-enquiry__grid {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  .fg-care-hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 9vw, 2.7rem); }

  .fg-care-hero__actions { display: grid; align-items: stretch; }
  .fg-care-hero__actions .button { width: 100%; text-align: center; }

  /* Both actions full width on the dark band too, since a phone number is the
     thing somebody standing at a stuck door actually wants. */
  .fg-care-callus__actions { display: grid; }
  .fg-care-callus__actions .button { width: 100%; text-align: center; }

  .fg-care-routine { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

  /* Two square tiles across at 390px gives roughly 165px each, which is enough
     for the photo to read and a comfortable tap target. One across would make
     the picker alone a four screen scroll before the guide even starts. */
  .fg-care-picker__cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .fg-care-card__label { padding: 0.6rem 0.65rem 0.65rem; }
  .fg-care-card__name { font-size: var(--fg-text-base); }
  .fg-care-card__covers { -webkit-line-clamp: 2; font-size: 0.7rem; }

  .fg-care-guide__hero { min-height: 180px; }
  .fg-care-guide { border-radius: 10px; }

  /* Button under the copy rather than stranded beside it. */
  .fg-care-callus { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .fg-care-callus .button { justify-self: start; }

  .fg-care-enquiry__form .fg-enquiry-form__compact-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .fg-care-enquiry__form .fg-enquiry-form__compact-wide { grid-column: auto; }
  .fg-care-enquiry__form .fg-enquiry-form__footer { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   Bi-fold configurations (/aluminium-bifold-doors/)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   One swipe rail of Sheerline's seventeen published layouts, with pane-count
   buttons that jump into it. NOT a configurator: it draws nothing, prices
   nothing, and hands over to the quote embed. See the component header in
   template-parts/components/bifold-configurations.php.

   IT SITS ON THE PAGE CANVAS, NOT A BAND. A dark band was tried on 2026-08-21
   and the owner pulled it: "on a dark background for no reason". It had been
   introduced to stop the doors floating, which was the wrong fix for the right
   problem. The floating was the renders carrying Sheerline's own #f2f2f2
   canvas baked in, within a few percent of our page gradient. Once that grey
   is keyed to transparency the doors read perfectly well on a light panel,
   checked against white, #f5f7f7, #eef2f3 and #e4ebed before choosing. Do not
   reach for a dark band here again.
   ========================================================================== */
.fg-bfc {
  padding: clamp(3rem, 5vw, 4.75rem) 0 clamp(2.5rem, 4vw, 3.75rem);
}

.fg-bfc__head {
  max-width: 60ch;
  margin-bottom: var(--fg-space-8);
}

.fg-bfc__head h2 {
  font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 2.2vw, 2rem);
  margin: var(--fg-space-2) 0 var(--fg-space-4);
}

.fg-bfc__head p {
  color: var(--color-steel-90);
  margin: 0 0 var(--fg-space-3);
}

.fg-bfc__aside {
  font-size: var(--fg-text-md);
  color: var(--color-muted);
}

/* `[hidden]` from the UA stylesheet loses to any class rule that sets
   `display`, and this row is a flex container. Without the guard the controls
   render before the controller has wired them. This has shipped broken twice
   on this site (the case-study Show more button and the repairs schematics). */
.fg-bfc__controls[hidden] { display: none !important; }

.fg-bfc__controls {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: var(--fg-space-4);
  margin-bottom: var(--fg-space-5);
}

.fg-bfc__jumps { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--fg-space-2); }

.fg-bfc__jump {
  min-width: 44px;
  min-height: 44px;
  padding: 0 var(--fg-space-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: #fff;
  color: var(--color-steel);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-lg);
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: border-color 0.18s ease, background 0.18s ease, color 0.18s ease;
}

.fg-bfc__jump:hover { border-color: var(--color-accent); }

.fg-bfc__jump[aria-pressed='true'] {
  background: var(--color-steel);
  border-color: var(--color-steel);
  color: #fff;
}

.fg-bfc__jump:focus-visible,
.fg-bfc__rail:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
  outline-offset: 3px;
  border-radius: 12px;
}

.fg-bfc__jumps-label {
  margin-left: var(--fg-space-1);
  font-size: var(--fg-text-2xs);
  letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--color-muted);
}

.fg-bfc__counter {
  /* No-op while the row fits on one line; when the counter wraps below the
     buttons on a phone this keeps it against the right edge instead of
     stranding it under them. */
  margin: 0 0 0 auto;
  font-size: var(--fg-text-sm);
  letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  color: var(--color-muted);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}

.fg-bfc__counter-sep { opacity: 0.45; margin: 0 0.15rem; }

/* THE RAIL IS THE SCROLLER AND THE GRID, on one element. Nesting a grid inside
   a separate overflow container makes the columns resolve against the parent's
   width and collapse to fit: seventeen slides came out 47px wide on desktop
   and 2px on a phone. `grid-auto-columns` on the scroller itself resolves
   against the VISIBLE width, which is the fix the colour rail and the
   obscured-glass picker already carry. Do not split these again.

   It sits outside `.container` so a card bleeds off the right edge of the
   viewport. That cut card is the affordance: a rail with a clean edge does not
   say it scrolls. The inline padding puts the first card back on the container
   line so it aligns with the heading above it. */
.fg-bfc__rail {
  --fg-bfc-gap: clamp(1rem, 1.8vw, 1.5rem);
  --fg-bfc-per-view: 2.2;
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0.25rem max(1rem, calc((100% - var(--container)) / 2)) 0.75rem;
  display: grid;
  grid-auto-flow: column;
  grid-auto-columns: calc((100% - (var(--fg-bfc-gap) * (var(--fg-bfc-per-view) - 1))) / var(--fg-bfc-per-view));
  gap: var(--fg-bfc-gap);
  overflow-x: auto;
  overflow-y: hidden;
  /* NO SCROLL SNAP, AND THAT IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE OWNER'S "not smooth
     scrolling" REPORT. Measured: with `proximity` a 200px wheel nudge on a
     523px card sits inside the snap zone of the card it started on, so the
     rail scrolled and then sprang straight back to 0. Every small push
     bounced. The colour rail gets away with proximity because its swatches
     are small enough that a nudge clears a boundary; these cards are not.
     Free scrolling here, and the jump buttons are what aligns a card when
     alignment is actually wanted. */
  /* NO `scroll-behavior: smooth` HERE, and its absence is the fix for the
     owner's "not smooth scrolling" report of 2026-08-21. Chrome applies that
     property to USER scrolling as well as scripted scrolling, so every wheel
     notch and every drag frame was animated towards its target instead of
     tracking the input, which reads as lag. The jump buttons pass
     `behavior: 'smooth'` to `scrollTo` themselves, which is the only place the
     animation was ever wanted. */
  scrollbar-width: none;
  -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
  overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
  min-width: 0;
  max-width: 100%;
}

.fg-bfc__rail::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }

.fg-bfc__rail.is-dragging {
  cursor: grabbing;
  user-select: none;
}

.fg-bfc__rail.is-dragging img { pointer-events: none; }

.fg-bfc__slide {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: var(--fg-space-4);
  min-width: 0;
}

/* A soft cool panel rather than white: the glass in these renders is very
   pale, so it needs something to be brighter than. Checked against four
   candidates before choosing. The hairline is there for the same reason the
   smooth white swatch and every handle tile carry one. */
.fg-bfc__stage {
  position: relative;
  margin: 0;
  aspect-ratio: 1024 / 392;
  border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  border-radius: 12px;
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, #f2f6f7 0%, #e6edef 100%);
  overflow: hidden;
}

/* Every door stands on the same line. Without it a two pane door is a small
   object adrift in a wide box; with it the box reads as a plinth and the width
   difference between two panes and seven becomes the thing you notice.

   88.8% is where the threshold sits in the shared crop window (baseline 348 of
   392). If the canvas ever changes, change this in the same commit. */
.fg-bfc__stage::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  left: 7%;
  right: 7%;
  top: 88.8%;
  height: 1px;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(0, 45, 58, 0) 0%, rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.16) 16%, rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.16) 84%, rgba(0, 45, 58, 0) 100%);
}

/* `display: block` is load-bearing: an image is inline by default, sits on the
   text baseline, and the descender adds ~8px of dead space under every stage. */
.fg-bfc__stage img {
  position: relative;
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}

.fg-bfc__meta { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.35rem; }

.fg-bfc__code {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: var(--fg-text-2xl);
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--color-steel);
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}

.fg-bfc__name {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: var(--fg-text-sm);
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--color-accent-dark);
}

.fg-bfc__copy {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: var(--fg-text-md);
  color: var(--color-steel-90);
}

.fg-bfc__width {
  margin: 0.15rem 0 0;
  font-size: var(--fg-text-sm);
  color: var(--color-muted);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}

.fg-bfc__foot {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: var(--fg-space-5);
  margin-top: var(--fg-space-8);
  padding-top: var(--fg-space-6);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
}

.fg-bfc__note {
  flex: 1 1 32rem;
  margin: 0;
  font-size: var(--fg-text-sm);
  color: var(--color-muted);
}

@media (max-width: 1180px) {
  .fg-bfc__rail { --fg-bfc-per-view: 1.7; }
}

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  /* About one and a sixth cards, so the next one is visibly cut. */
  .fg-bfc__rail { --fg-bfc-per-view: 1.15; }

  .fg-bfc__head { margin-bottom: var(--fg-space-6); }
  .fg-bfc__controls { gap: var(--fg-space-3); }
  .fg-bfc__foot .button { width: 100%; justify-content: center; }
}

/* Hub -> showroom handover. The hub answers which product; the showroom
   answers what it actually looks like. Without this link the showroom is a
   real, indexable page that nothing on the site points at. */
.fg-product-hub__showroom-link {
  margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
  font-size: 0.92rem;

  a {
    color: var(--color-accent-dark);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Composite door style range — `.fg-cds`
 *
 * Replaces the drifting `.fg-cd3-wall`. That was a decorative band of 33
 * photographs under a claim of "over 300 door styles"; this is the 142 doors
 * the quote tool can actually price, as line art, each one a link that opens
 * the tool on that door.
 *
 * THE `[hidden]` GUARDS ARE NOT OPTIONAL. This component sets `display` on the
 * switcher and the panels, and an author rule outranks the UA sheet's
 * `[hidden] { display: none }`. Without these the switcher ships visible before
 * the controller runs and every panel shows at once. That exact fault has now
 * caught the case-study archives, the repairs drawings and the bi-fold rail.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

.fg-cds {
  padding-block: clamp(48px, 6vw, 80px);
}

.fg-cds__switcher {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 8px;
  margin-block: 28px 24px;

  &[hidden] { display: none !important; }
}

.fg-cds__tab {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8px;
  min-height: 44px;
  padding: 8px 16px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.18);
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: #fff;
  color: var(--color-steel, #1d2a2f);
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background-color 0.18s ease, border-color 0.18s ease, color 0.18s ease;

  &:hover { border-color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.4); }

  &[aria-selected='true'] {
    background: var(--color-steel, #1d2a2f);
    border-color: var(--color-steel, #1d2a2f);
    color: #fff;
  }
}

.fg-cds__tab-count {
  font-size: 0.78rem;
  font-weight: 500;
  opacity: 0.65;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}

.fg-cds__panel {
  &[hidden] { display: none !important; }

  + .fg-cds__panel { margin-top: 40px; }
}

/* With the switcher live the panel heading is redundant — the selected tab
   already names the collection — so it is hidden visually but kept for screen
   readers, which is also what the no-JS version needs. */
.fg-cds.is-enhanced .fg-cds__panel-title {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  padding: 0;
  margin: -1px;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip-path: inset(50%);
  white-space: nowrap;
}

.fg-cds__panel-title {
  margin: 0 0 16px;
  font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 1.6vw, 1.35rem);
}

.fg-cds__grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(112px, 1fr));
  gap: clamp(14px, 2vw, 24px);
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.fg-cds-door__link {
  display: block;
  text-decoration: none;
  color: inherit;

  &:hover .fg-cds-door__art,
  &:focus-visible .fg-cds-door__art {
    border-color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.42);
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }

  &:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent, #2eac66);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }
}

.fg-cds-door__art {
  display: block;
  padding: 10px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.14);
  border-radius: 6px;
  background: #fff;
  transition: border-color 0.18s ease, transform 0.18s ease;

  img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    /* The drawings are stroked in `currentColor`, so the ink follows the page
       rather than being baked black. */
    color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.82);
  }
}

.fg-cds-door__name {
  display: block;
  margin-top: 8px;
  font-size: 0.8rem;
  line-height: 1.3;
  text-align: center;
  color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.72);
}

.fg-cds__note {
  margin-top: 32px;
  max-width: 62ch;
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.72);
}

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .fg-cds__grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(92px, 1fr));
  }

  .fg-cds__switcher {
    /* A row of six pills wraps to three lines on a phone and eats the section.
       Scrolled sideways it stays one line and the clipped pill says so. */
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    overflow-x: auto;
    scrollbar-width: none;
    margin-inline: calc(var(--fg-gutter, 20px) * -1);
    padding-inline: var(--fg-gutter, 20px);

    &::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }
  }

  .fg-cds__tab { flex: 0 0 auto; }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .fg-cds-door__art,
  .fg-cds__tab { transition: none; }

  .fg-cds-door__link:hover .fg-cds-door__art { transform: none; }
}

/* The two lines a swatch cannot carry, added with the glass section on
   2026-08-26. `.fg-composite-glass` and its cards were already styled; these
   are the range statement and the aperture caveat that sit under the grid. */
.fg-composite-glass__aperture,
.fg-composite-glass__range {
  max-width: 68ch;
  margin: 20px 0 0;
  font-size: 1rem;
  line-height: 1.6;
  color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.78);
}

.fg-composite-glass__aperture {
  margin-top: 32px;
  padding-left: 16px;
  border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent, #2eac66);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Composite door quiz — `.fg-cdq`
 *
 * A WHITE PANEL WITH AN EDGE, SITTING ON THE PAGE CANVAS. It was built on a
 * dark full-bleed stage first and that was the wrong call: the page around it
 * is a continuous pale gradient, and dropping a black band through the middle
 * of it cut the page in half rather than reading as a feature. `STYLE.md` is
 * explicit that the canvas is continuous and a solid band needs a clear reason;
 * this did not have one.
 *
 * The card is the reason instead — one bordered white surface that holds the
 * whole quiz, so it reads as a self-contained thing you step into without
 * repainting the page underneath it.
 *
 * It is still built to be shared: one question at a time at display size, and
 * the answers are real door drawings rather than icons, so nobody has to
 * imagine what "a decent amount of glass" means.
 *
 * The `[hidden]` guards are not optional. This component sets `display` on the
 * steps and the result, and an author rule outranks the UA sheet's
 * `[hidden] { display: none }` — the fault that has now caught the case-study
 * archives, the repairs drawings and the bi-fold rail controls.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

.fg-cdq { padding-block: clamp(44px, 6vw, 80px); }

/* THE WHITE CARD IS GONE, 2026-08-27. The quiz is a full-bleed dark band now
   and a white panel inside it hid the whole thing: the heading is white type,
   so it vanished, and the answer tiles lost the contrast that makes them read
   as tiles. Kept as a no-op rather than deleted so the shape of what was here
   is still legible next to the rules below it, which still apply. */
.fg-cdq > .container {
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 0;
  background: none;
  box-shadow: none;
}

.fg-cdq__head {
  max-width: 60ch;
  margin-bottom: clamp(24px, 3.4vw, 40px);

  h2 {
    margin: 8px 0 12px;
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.6vw, 2.7rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.3vw, 1.1rem);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.76);
  }
}

.fg-cdq__panel { &[hidden] { display: none !important; } }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------- progress */

.fg-cdq__progress {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8px;
  margin-bottom: clamp(20px, 3vw, 30px);
}

.fg-cdq__pip {
  width: 34px;
  height: 4px;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.14);
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease;

  &.is-done { background: var(--color-accent, #2eac66); }
}

.fg-cdq__count {
  margin-left: 8px;
  font-size: 0.78rem;
  letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.55);
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------- questions */

.fg-cdq__q {
  animation: fg-cdq-in 0.4s cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.61, 0.36, 1) both;

  &[hidden] { display: none !important; }
}

@keyframes fg-cdq-in {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(12px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}

.fg-cdq__kicker {
  margin: 0 0 6px;
  font-size: 0.76rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.09em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--color-accent, #2eac66);
}

.fg-cdq__title {
  margin: 0 0 clamp(20px, 3vw, 32px);
  max-width: 24ch;
  font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.2vw, 2.5rem);
  line-height: 1.1;
  text-wrap: balance;
}

.fg-cdq__answers {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(170px, 1fr));
  gap: clamp(12px, 1.6vw, 20px);
}

.fg-cdq__answers--4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr)); }
.fg-cdq__answers--2 { grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(230px, 1fr)); max-width: 720px; }

.fg-cdq__answer {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 14px;
  min-height: 44px;
  padding: clamp(12px, 1.6vw, 18px);
  border: 1px solid rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.16);
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: #fff;
  color: inherit;
  text-align: left;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: border-color 0.18s ease, background-color 0.18s ease, transform 0.18s ease, box-shadow 0.18s ease;

  &:hover,
  &:focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--color-accent, #2eac66);
    background: rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.06);
    transform: translateY(-3px);
    box-shadow: 0 12px 26px rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.09);
  }

  &:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent, #2eac66); outline-offset: 3px; }
}

/* The "skip this one" answer carries no drawing, so it should not reserve the
   height of one and sit as a tall empty box beside the illustrated pair. */
.fg-cdq__answer--plain { justify-content: flex-end; }

/* The drawing sits on white with its own edge. An SVG loaded through `<img src>`
   is an isolated document — it inherits nothing from this page, so
   `currentColor` inside resolves to its own black and no amount of `color` here
   changes it. Black on white is what these are drawn for. */
.fg-cdq__answer-art {
  display: block;
  padding: 10px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.12);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: #fff;

  img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 92px;
    height: auto;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
}

.fg-cdq__answer-text {
  display: block;

  strong { display: block; font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.25; }
  small  { display: block; margin-top: 3px; font-size: 0.84rem; line-height: 1.35; color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.62); }
}

.fg-cdq__back {
  margin-top: 20px;
  padding: 8px 4px;
  border: 0;
  background: none;
  color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.62);
  font-size: 0.88rem;
  text-decoration: underline;
  cursor: pointer;

  &[hidden] { display: none !important; }
  &:hover { color: var(--color-steel, #1d2a2f); }
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------- the reveal */

.fg-cdq__result {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 7fr);
  gap: clamp(22px, 3.4vw, 44px);
  align-items: start;
  animation: fg-cdq-reveal 0.5s cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.61, 0.36, 1) both;

  &[hidden] { display: none !important; }
}

@keyframes fg-cdq-reveal {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.98) translateY(14px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}

.fg-cdq__reveal-kicker {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 0.8rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.09em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--color-accent, #2eac66);
}

.fg-cdq__door-name {
  margin: 6px 0 4px;
  font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.2vw, 3.2rem);
  line-height: 1.04;
}

.fg-cdq__collection {
  margin: 0 0 20px;
  font-size: 0.94rem;
  color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.6);
}

.fg-cdq__art {
  margin: 0 0 18px;
  padding: 22px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.14);
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: #fff;

  img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 175px;
    height: auto;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
}

.fg-cdq__why {
  margin: 0 0 14px;
  font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.3vw, 1.1rem);
  line-height: 1.55;
}

.fg-cdq__poa {
  margin: 0 0 14px;
  padding-left: 14px;
  border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent, #2eac66);
  font-size: 0.92rem;
  line-height: 1.5;
  color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.72);

  &[hidden] { display: none !important; }
}

.fg-cdq__result-actions {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 10px;
  margin: 0 0 16px;
}

.fg-cdq__caveat {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 0.86rem;
  line-height: 1.5;
  color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.6);
}

.fg-cdq__frame {
  position: relative;
  aspect-ratio: 3 / 4;
  min-height: 460px;
  overflow: hidden;
  border: 1px solid rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.16);
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: #f5f7f7;

  iframe { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; border: 0; }
}

.fg-cdq__frame-note {
  margin: 10px 0 0;
  font-size: 0.85rem;
  color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.6);
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------- responsive */

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .fg-cdq__result { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .fg-cdq__frame { aspect-ratio: 4 / 5; min-height: 480px; }
  .fg-cdq__answers,
  .fg-cdq__answers--4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(140px, 1fr)); }
  .fg-cdq__answer-art img { max-width: 78px; }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .fg-cdq__q,
  .fg-cdq__result { animation: none; }

  .fg-cdq__answer { transition: none; }
  .fg-cdq__answer:hover { transform: none; }
}
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * `/why-distinction/` — `.fg-wd-*`
 * A support page for the composite range: reading, not browsing, so it is one
 * column with a comfortable measure rather than a grid of tiles.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

.fg-wd-page section { padding-block: clamp(44px, 5.5vw, 76px); }

.fg-wd-hero {
  h1 { margin: 6px 0 14px; font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 3.6vw, var(--fg-font-size-max, 3.6rem)); }
}

.fg-wd-lead { max-width: 62ch; font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.4vw, 1.18rem); line-height: 1.6; }

.fg-wd-hero__actions,
.fg-wd-judgement__actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px; margin-top: 22px; }

.fg-wd-layers {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
  gap: clamp(16px, 2.4vw, 26px);
  margin: 28px 0 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
  counter-reset: none;
}

.fg-wd-layer {
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.12);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);

  h3 { margin: 8px 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  p  { margin: 0; font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.55; color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.8); }
}

.fg-wd-layer__num {
  font-size: 0.74rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  color: var(--color-accent, #2eac66);
}

.fg-wd-figure {
  p { max-width: 66ch; line-height: 1.6; }
}

.fg-wd-stat {
  margin: 18px 0 16px;
  font-size: 1.1rem;
  font-weight: 600;

  span {
    display: block;
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 5vw, var(--fg-font-size-max, 3.6rem));
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--color-accent, #2eac66);
  }
}

.fg-wd-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
  gap: clamp(16px, 2.4vw, 26px);
  margin-top: 26px;
}

.fg-wd-card {
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.12);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: #fff;

  h3 { margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.1rem; }
  p  { margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.55; color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.8); }
}

.fg-wd-judgement p { max-width: 66ch; line-height: 1.6; }

/* The through-link to `/why-distinction/` on the composite page. A band, not a
   section: it is a signpost, and the page it points at carries the detail. */
.fg-wd-cta { padding-block: clamp(36px, 4vw, 56px); }

.fg-wd-cta__inner {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
  gap: clamp(18px, 3vw, 36px);
  align-items: center;
  padding: clamp(22px, 3vw, 34px);
  border: 1px solid rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.12);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);

  h2 { margin: 6px 0 10px; font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 2.2vw, 2rem); }
  p  { margin: 0; max-width: 62ch; line-height: 1.55; color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.8); }
}

.fg-wd-cta__action { margin: 0; }

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .fg-wd-cta__inner { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * `/composite-doors/` — page rhythm
 *
 * THE PROBLEM THIS FIXES WAS MEASURED, NOT FELT. The page runs to 13,355px
 * across eighteen sections and every one of them sat transparent on the same
 * pale canvas, with vertical padding that had drifted to 40, 42.75, 44, 48, 56
 * and 80px with no system behind it — and one section resolving to 0 because a
 * plain class lost to a more specific selector. `STYLE.md` asks for deliberate
 * variation between narrative and utility sections; what was there was not
 * variation, it was accident.
 *
 * THE RULE IS: IF YOU ARE CHOOSING, IT IS ON A CARD. IF WE ARE TELLING YOU
 * SOMETHING, IT IS OPEN ON THE CANVAS. The style range, the quiz and the glass
 * are the three sections where a visitor is picking, and they now share one
 * surface so they read as a set. The collections, the slab, the security
 * guarantee and the through-link stay open, so the page alternates between
 * "here is something to look at" and "here is something to do" instead of
 * running flat for thirteen thousand pixels.
 *
 * SCOPED TO THIS PAGE ONLY, through the slug class the generated-page template
 * already emits. `.generated-page--composite-doors > section` is (0,1,1), which
 * is what lets it take the padding back off the individual components — the
 * same specificity trap `AI.md` records against `.fg-product-hub > section`,
 * used deliberately this time rather than tripped over.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

.generated-page--composite-doors {

  /* Major narrative sections. STYLE.md puts a major handoff around 72-80px. */
  > section { padding-block: clamp(52px, 6vw, 84px); }

  /* Compact utility strips: a signpost, a badge row, a finish grid. These are
     punctuation between chapters and should not breathe like one. */
  > .fg-cd3-brief,
  > .fg-composite-approved,
  > .fg-wd-cta,
  > .fg-handle-finishes { padding-block: clamp(32px, 3.6vw, 52px); }

  /* The hero sets its own composition and must not be padded from outside. */
  > .fg-cd3-hero { padding-block: 0; }

  /* The three "you are choosing" chapters, on one shared surface. The quiz
     already carries this and the glass grid carries a version of it; the style
     range was bare, which is why the top third of the page read as one
     undifferentiated scroll. */
  > .fg-cds > .container {
    padding: clamp(24px, 3.6vw, 52px);
    border: 1px solid rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.14);
    border-radius: 10px;
    background: #fff;
    box-shadow: var(--fg-shadow-lg);
  }
}

/* The style range and the quiz are one thought — see the whole range, then let
   us narrow it — so they sit closer together than two unrelated sections would.
   Without this the quiz reads as a separate feature that happens to follow. */
.generated-page--composite-doors > .fg-cds { padding-bottom: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 32px); }
.generated-page--composite-doors > .fg-cdq { padding-top: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 32px); }

/* The colour step. Eight chips rather than eight door drawings — the question is
   about the colour, so the colour is the whole answer and a door underneath it
   would only compete. Chips carry an edge because Cream and White are all but
   invisible on a white card without one, the same reason the smooth-white swatch
   on the colour hub has always needed a border. */
.fg-cdq__answers--swatches {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(122px, 1fr));
}

.fg-cdq__answer--swatch {
  gap: 10px;
  padding: clamp(10px, 1.3vw, 14px);
}

.fg-cdq__chip {
  display: block;
  aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
  border: 1px solid rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.22);
  border-radius: 6px;
}

.fg-cdq__chosen-colour {
  margin: -14px 0 20px;
  font-size: 0.94rem;
  color: rgba(29, 42, 47, 0.6);

  &[hidden] { display: none !important; }
}

/* ==================================================================
 * COMPOSITE DOORS, 2026-08-27 OVERHAUL
 *
 * The page ran to 13,909px across seventeen sections at 1440x900, every
 * page `h2` resolved to 28.8px, and seven sections were taller than a
 * full desktop viewport. Nothing was allowed to be more important than
 * anything else, so nothing led. This block gives the page five
 * chapters, a hero that owns the fold, and the slab cutaway from the
 * design handoff.
 *
 * IT IS APPENDED RATHER THAN WOVEN IN, on purpose. `PROGRESS.md`
 * records a session that implemented "delete this block" as "truncate
 * from here" and silently destroyed the `/why-distinction/` styles for
 * three commits. Everything new is here, in one place, where equal
 * specificity resolves in its favour by document order, and the rules
 * above are edited only where they would actively fight it.
 * ================================================================== */

/* ---- Chapter heads ------------------------------------------------
 * Fourteen identical 28.8px headings is why the page read flat.
 * `STYLE.md` allows an art-directed product section to rise toward
 * 2.5rem, so a chapter opener does and the steps inside it drop to h3.
 * Nothing here goes near `--fg-font-size-max`.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

.fg-chapter-head {
  max-width: 46rem;
  margin: 0 0 clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.4rem);

  .eyebrow { margin: 0 0 0.55rem; }

  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 2.9vw, 2.35rem);
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
  }

  > p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 58ch;
    color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.72);
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
}

/* ---- Chapter one: the hero ---------------------------------------
 * It was 514px inside a 900px viewport, so the fold showed the hero, a
 * four-fact strip and the top of an installer badge: three stacked
 * bands rather than one door. Both strips are inside it now.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero {
  display: grid;
  align-content: center;
  gap: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.6rem);
  /* Fills the fold under the 72px header without forcing empty space on
     a short screen, and never taller than the content needs. */
  min-height: min(830px, calc(100svh - 76px));
  padding-block: clamp(1.5rem, 3vh, 2.75rem);
}

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero__grid {
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 0.86fr);
  gap: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
  align-items: center;
}

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero h1 {
  max-width: 13ch;
  margin-bottom: 1rem;
}

.fg-cd3-hero__credential {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.9rem;
  max-width: 44ch;
  margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
  padding-top: 1.25rem;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.12);

  img {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 104px;
    height: auto;
  }

  span {
    color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.64);
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
}

/* The photograph is portrait and ours. Held at its own proportion so
   nothing crops the door or the sidelight out of frame. */
.fg-cd3-hero__media {
  margin: 0;

  img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: min(620px, calc(100svh - 220px));
    border-radius: 10px;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 42%;
    box-shadow: 0 30px 70px -34px rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.55), 0 2px 6px rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.1);
  }
}

/* The four facts, on the hero. `142` replaced `Six collections`, which
   was a navigation note in a slot the other three fill with a number. */
.fg-cd3-hero__facts {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
  margin: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  border: 1px solid rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.12);
  border-radius: 12px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
  backdrop-filter: blur(10px);

  > div { padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem; }
  > div + div { border-left: 1px solid rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.1); }

  dt {
    margin: 0 0 0.2rem;
    color: var(--fg-ink, #022b35);
    font-size: 1.28rem;
    font-weight: 750;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.66);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }
}

/* ---- Chapter two: choosing ---------------------------------------
 * The range and the quiz were two white cards with a seam between
 * them. They are one thought and now one surface.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

.fg-choose__surface {
  padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3.4vw, 2.75rem);
  border: 1px solid rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.13);
  border-radius: 16px;
  background: #ffffff;
  box-shadow: 0 26px 66px -34px rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.34);
}

.fg-choose__rule {
  height: 1px;
  margin: clamp(1.75rem, 3.4vw, 2.75rem) 0;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.14), rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.04));
}

/* Bare mode: the surface above supplies the container and the padding. */
.fg-cds--bare,
.fg-cdq--bare { padding: 0 !important; }

/* The chapter opener rises; the quiz beneath it stays a normal h2 so
   the two read as opener and step rather than as two equal sections. */
.fg-cds__head h2 {
  font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 2.9vw, 2.35rem);
  line-height: 1.08;
  letter-spacing: -0.012em;
}

/* The slab line and description each collection used to carry on its
   own carousel card, now inside the panel its tab opens. */
.fg-cds__panel-head {
  max-width: 60ch;
  margin: 0 0 clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
}

.fg-cds__panel-title {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: baseline;
  gap: 0.35rem 0.7rem;
  margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
  font-size: 1.16rem;
}

.fg-cds__panel-slab {
  color: var(--fg-green);
  font-size: 0.78rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}

.fg-cds__panel-intro {
  margin: 0;
  color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.7);
  font-size: 0.94rem;
  line-height: 1.55;
}

/* FIVE COLUMNS IS A COUNTING DECISION, NOT A TASTE ONE. The six
   collections hold 19, 43, 27, 25, 23 and 5 doors, and five is the only
   desktop column count where none of them leaves a remainder of one, so
   no collection strands a single door beside four empty cells the way
   19-in-eights did. Re-check that before changing it. */
.fg-cds__grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: clamp(0.6rem, 1.4vw, 1rem);
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.fg-cds-door__link {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-rows: 1fr auto auto;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  height: 100%;
  padding: 0.75rem 0.75rem 0.65rem;
  border: 1px solid rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.11);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: #ffffff;
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
  transition: border-color 160ms ease, box-shadow 160ms ease, transform 160ms ease;
}

.fg-cds-door__art {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-height: 0;
}

/* THE DRAWINGS ARE SET BACK WITH OPACITY, NOT RECOLOURED. They are
   `fill:none; stroke:currentColor` loaded through `<img src>`, which is
   an isolated document, so `currentColor` resolves to their own black
   and nothing set on the `<img>` reaches inside. They also carry their
   stroke weights inline, 55.9 on the frame and 28 on the panels against
   a 914 viewBox, which is what made eight columns of them read as a
   wall of black. Opacity is the one lever that works here without a
   mask, and unlike `mask-image` it degrades to the drawing rather than
   to a solid block. */
.fg-cds-door__link img {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
  max-height: 190px;
  object-fit: contain;
  opacity: 0.66;
  transition: opacity 160ms ease;
}

/* Two lines reserved so a wrapping name cannot push its neighbours out
   of line, which is what broke the row rhythm at eight columns. */
.fg-cds-door__name {
  display: block;
  min-height: 2.5em;
  color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.78);
  font-size: 0.8rem;
  line-height: 1.25;
  text-align: center;
}

/* THE AFFORDANCE THE SECTION WAS MISSING. Every drawing opens the quote
   tool on that door and nothing on the card said so. */
.fg-cds-door__cue {
  display: block;
  color: var(--fg-green);
  font-size: 0.68rem;
  font-weight: 750;
  letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  text-align: center;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 160ms ease;
}

.fg-cds-door__link:hover,
.fg-cds-door__link:focus-visible {
  border-color: rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.5);
  box-shadow: 0 14px 30px -18px rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.4);
  transform: translateY(-2px);

  img { opacity: 1; }
  .fg-cds-door__cue { opacity: 1; }
}

.fg-cds__note {
  max-width: 68ch;
  margin: clamp(1.1rem, 2.2vw, 1.6rem) 0 0;
  color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.68);
  font-size: 0.93rem;
  line-height: 1.6;
}

/* A SKIP IS NOT AN ANSWER. "Honestly, neither" carries no drawing, so
   inside the answer grid it rendered as an empty bordered box the same
   size as two illustrated doors, which reads as a missing image. */
.fg-cdq__skip {
  margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
  text-align: center;
}

.fg-cdq__skip-btn {
  padding: 0.4rem 0.9rem;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: transparent;
  color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.6);
  font: inherit;
  font-size: 0.88rem;
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-underline-offset: 3px;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: color 150ms ease, background 150ms ease;
}

.fg-cdq__skip-btn:hover,
.fg-cdq__skip-btn:focus-visible {
  background: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.05);
  color: var(--fg-ink, #022b35);
}

/* ---- Chapter three: the slab, cut open ---------------------------
 * Built from `design_handoff_composite_door_anatomy/`. Every number is
 * a percentage of a box with `aspect-ratio: 0.43`, so the drawing
 * scales with its column and there is not a pixel in it.
 *
 * The cutaway is TWO concentric arcs: a large one removes the skin, a
 * smaller one also removes the board, so moving into the top-right
 * corner reads skin, then board, then foam.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

.fg-cd3-anatomy {
  --slab-skin-arc: ellipse(56% 78% at 100% 0);
  --slab-board-arc: ellipse(36% 50% at 100% 0);
  --slab-green: #2eac66;
  --slab-ease: cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.7, 0.2, 1);

  /* Holes. The shape walks out to the hole along a zero-width slit,
     traces it, and walks back. */
  --slab-skin-hole: polygon(0% 0%, 20% 0%, 20% 15%, 62% 15%, 62% 33%, 20% 33%, 20% 15%, 20% 0%, 100% 0%, 100% 100%, 0% 100%);
  --slab-core-hole: polygon(0% 0%, 17.4% 0%, 17.4% 13.882%, 64.6% 13.882%, 64.6% 34.118%, 17.4% 34.118%, 17.4% 13.882%, 17.4% 0%, 100% 0%, 100% 100%, 0% 100%);
  --slab-glass-clip: inset(15% 38% 67% 20%);

  /* Procedural materials: feTurbulence as a data URI, tiled. They cost
     nothing to serve and scale cleanly. */
  --slab-foam: url("data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns=%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%20width=%22200%22%20height=%22200%22%3E%3Cfilter%20id=%22f%22%3E%3CfeTurbulence%20type=%22turbulence%22%20baseFrequency=%220.055%22%20numOctaves=%224%22%20seed=%227%22%20stitchTiles=%22stitch%22%20result=%22t%22/%3E%3CfeDiffuseLighting%20in=%22t%22%20lighting-color=%22%23cdd0ce%22%20surfaceScale=%223%22%3E%3CfeDistantLight%20azimuth=%22235%22%20elevation=%2256%22/%3E%3C/feDiffuseLighting%3E%3C/filter%3E%3Crect%20width=%22200%22%20height=%22200%22%20filter=%22url%28%23f%29%22/%3E%3C/svg%3E");
  --slab-board: url("data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns=%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%20width=%22180%22%20height=%22180%22%3E%3Cfilter%20id=%22f%22%3E%3CfeTurbulence%20type=%22fractalNoise%22%20baseFrequency=%220.85%22%20numOctaves=%225%22%20seed=%223%22%20stitchTiles=%22stitch%22%20result=%22t%22/%3E%3CfeColorMatrix%20in=%22t%22%20type=%22saturate%22%20values=%220%22%20result=%22m%22/%3E%3CfeComponentTransfer%20in=%22m%22%3E%3CfeFuncR%20type=%22linear%22%20slope=%220.36%22%20intercept=%220.63%22/%3E%3CfeFuncG%20type=%22linear%22%20slope=%220.36%22%20intercept=%220.59%22/%3E%3CfeFuncB%20type=%22linear%22%20slope=%220.36%22%20intercept=%220.5%22/%3E%3CfeFuncA%20type=%22linear%22%20slope=%220%22%20intercept=%221%22/%3E%3C/feComponentTransfer%3E%3C/filter%3E%3Crect%20width=%22180%22%20height=%22180%22%20filter=%22url%28%23f%29%22/%3E%3C/svg%3E");
  --slab-wood: url("data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns=%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%20width=%22120%22%20height=%22300%22%3E%3Cfilter%20id=%22f%22%3E%3CfeTurbulence%20type=%22fractalNoise%22%20baseFrequency=%220.9%200.012%22%20numOctaves=%224%22%20seed=%2211%22%20stitchTiles=%22stitch%22%20result=%22t%22/%3E%3CfeColorMatrix%20in=%22t%22%20type=%22saturate%22%20values=%220%22%20result=%22m%22/%3E%3CfeComponentTransfer%20in=%22m%22%3E%3CfeFuncR%20type=%22linear%22%20slope=%220.46%22%20intercept=%220.24%22/%3E%3CfeFuncG%20type=%22linear%22%20slope=%220.46%22%20intercept=%220.14%22/%3E%3CfeFuncB%20type=%22linear%22%20slope=%220.46%22%20intercept=%220.06%22/%3E%3CfeFuncA%20type=%22linear%22%20slope=%220%22%20intercept=%221%22/%3E%3C/feComponentTransfer%3E%3C/filter%3E%3Crect%20width=%22120%22%20height=%22300%22%20filter=%22url%28%23f%29%22/%3E%3C/svg%3E");
}

/* The figure is a crop. The slab is full height inside it and pulled up
   so the cut corner sits in frame, which is what the old bitmap's
   `object-position: center 30%` was doing. */
.fg-cd3-anatomy__media { position: relative; }

.fg-cd3-slab {
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  top: -8%;
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 0.43;
}

.fg-cd3-slab__shadow {
  position: absolute;
  right: -12%;
  bottom: -3.2%;
  left: -12%;
  height: 4.6%;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: radial-gradient(50% 50% at 50% 50%, rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.36), rgba(0, 45, 58, 0) 72%);
  filter: blur(3px);
}

.fg-cd3-slab__body {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  border-radius: 3px;
  box-shadow: 0 26px 50px -26px rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.6), 0 2px 5px rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.2);
}

.fg-cd3-slab__stack {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 0.43;
}

/* Reset before anything positions an image inside the drawing. The
   `.fg-cd3-anatomy__media img` rule earlier in this file is (0,1,1) and
   would otherwise reach in; this matches it and wins on document
   order. */
.fg-cd3-slab img {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
  object-position: center;
}

/* THE APERTURE IS CUT CLEAN THROUGH BOARD AND FOAM. No foam runs behind
   the glass; the engineered-wood surround frames it. */
.fg-cd3-slab__core {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  clip-path: var(--slab-core-hole);
}

.fg-cd3-slab__foam {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 7% 8.6%;
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.26)), var(--slab-foam);
  background-size: auto, 200px 200px;
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 26px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.32);
}

.fg-cd3-slab__board {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 7% 8.6%;
  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 36% 50% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 99.4%, #000 100%);
  mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 36% 50% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 99.4%, #000 100%);
  -webkit-mask-size: cover;
  mask-size: cover;
}

.fg-cd3-slab__board-mat {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2)), var(--slab-board);
  background-size: auto, 180px 180px;
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 22px rgba(70, 60, 45, 0.32);
}

.fg-cd3-slab__board-arc {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: radial-gradient(ellipse 36% 50% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 98.6%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4) 99.4%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85) 100%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 100.4%);
}

.fg-cd3-slab__surround {
  position: absolute;
  top: 13.882%;
  left: 17.4%;
  width: 47.2%;
  height: 20.236%;
}

.fg-cd3-slab__surround-mat {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background-image: linear-gradient(150deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.34)), var(--slab-wood);
  background-size: auto, 120px 300px;
}

/* The reveal where the cassette beds into the timber. These insets are
   a share of the FRAME box, not the slab, because that is what
   percentages resolve against here. */
.fg-cd3-slab__surround-reveal {
  position: absolute;
  top: 5.5248%;
  right: 5.5085%;
  bottom: 5.5248%;
  left: 5.5085%;
  box-shadow: 0 0 5px 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* TIMBER SITS INSIDE POLYMER. The polymer rail is what the weather
   touches; the engineered wood is a reinforcement behind it that never
   sees daylight. Distinction's own graphic has this the wrong way
   round. Do not "fix" it back. */
.fg-cd3-slab__timber i,
.fg-cd3-slab__poly i { position: absolute; display: block; }

.fg-cd3-slab__timber {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;

  i { background-size: auto, 120px 300px; }

  .is-l {
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    left: 3.4%;
    width: 5.2%;
    background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16) 46%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.34)), var(--slab-wood);
  }

  .is-r {
    top: 0;
    right: 3.4%;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 5.2%;
    background-image: linear-gradient(270deg, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16) 46%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.34)), var(--slab-wood);
  }

  .is-t {
    top: 2.2%;
    right: 3.4%;
    left: 3.4%;
    height: 4.4%;
    background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.42)), var(--slab-wood);
  }

  .is-b {
    right: 3.4%;
    bottom: 2.2%;
    left: 3.4%;
    height: 4.6%;
    background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.42), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16)), var(--slab-wood);
  }
}

.fg-cd3-slab__poly {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;

  i { background-color: #e7e5df; }

  .is-l {
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 3.4%;
    background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.14));
    box-shadow: inset -1px 0 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.22);
  }

  .is-r {
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 3.4%;
    background-image: linear-gradient(270deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.14));
    box-shadow: inset 1px 0 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.22);
  }

  .is-t {
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
    left: 0;
    height: 2.2%;
    background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.12));
  }

  .is-b {
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    left: 0;
    height: 2.2%;
    background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.12), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9));
  }
}

/* The GRP skin: holed at the cassette, cut by the big arc, carrying the
   door photograph. */
.fg-cd3-slab__skin {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  clip-path: var(--slab-skin-hole);
}

.fg-cd3-slab__skin-mask {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 56% 78% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 99.5%, #000 100%);
  mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 56% 78% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 99.5%, #000 100%);
  -webkit-mask-size: cover;
  mask-size: cover;
}

/* Sampled from the bitmap itself, so the cassette's frame genuinely
   matches the slab it is set into. */
.fg-cd3-slab__skin-base {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: #7f8484;
}

.fg-cd3-slab__face { position: absolute; inset: 0; }

.fg-cd3-slab__sheen {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(105deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0) 36%),
    linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0) 74%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.13));
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* The bright edge of the cut. */
.fg-cd3-slab__cut {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: radial-gradient(ellipse 56% 78% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 98.8%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.45) 99.5%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9) 100%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 100.5%);
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* THE SEALED UNIT, READ EDGE-ON where the arc saws through it: three
   panes and two cavities. TWO TRAPS, both of which cost time in design.
   It must be a SIBLING of the glass face, never a child, because the
   two masks are exact complements and nesting them multiplies the alpha
   to zero so the sawn edge silently disappears. And it must be a
   FULL-SLAB element clipped down to the cassette rect, not an element
   sized to the cassette, or the arc mask resolves against the wrong box
   and the ellipse lands somewhere else. */
.fg-cd3-slab__glass-sawn {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 9;
  inset: 0;
  clip-path: var(--slab-glass-clip);
  background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #7f8c93 0 14%, #eef4f6 14% 34%, #b9ccd5 34% 50%, #eef4f6 50% 70%, #b9ccd5 70% 86%, #7f8c93 86% 100%);
  background-repeat: repeat;
  background-size: 11px 100%;
  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 56% 78% at 100% 0, #000 99.5%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 100%);
  mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 56% 78% at 100% 0, #000 99.5%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 100%);
  -webkit-mask-size: cover;
  mask-size: cover;
  pointer-events: none;
}

.fg-cd3-slab__glass-clip {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 8;
  inset: 0;
  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 56% 78% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 99.5%, #000 100%);
  mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 56% 78% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 99.5%, #000 100%);
  -webkit-mask-size: cover;
  mask-size: cover;
}

.fg-cd3-slab__cassette {
  position: absolute;
  top: 15%;
  left: 20%;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  width: 42%;
  height: 18%;
  border: 5px solid #7f8484;
  box-shadow: 0 3px 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.34), inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
  transition: box-shadow 0.4s ease;
}

.fg-cd3-slab__glass-base {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background-image: linear-gradient(160deg, #e4edf0, #b9c9d3 58%, #d9e3e8);
}

/* `inset: 0` with no fit means `object-fit: fill`, which squashed a 458x464
   source into a square box. It was 307x307 and square before, so this never
   showed; it would have the moment anyone swapped the file. */
.fg-cd3-slab__glass-face { position: absolute; inset: 0; object-fit: cover; }

.fg-cd3-slab__lead {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  pointer-events: none;

  i {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 3px;
    background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #6c7278, #cbd0d4 42%, #787e84);
    box-shadow: 0 0 3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35);
    transform: translateX(-50%);
  }

  i:nth-child(1) { left: 25%; }
  i:nth-child(2) { left: 50%; }
  i:nth-child(3) { left: 75%; }

  b {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    width: 34%;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    border: 3px solid;
    border-image: linear-gradient(135deg, #6c7278, #cbd0d4 45%, #787e84) 1;
    box-shadow: 0 0 4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(45deg);
  }
}

.fg-cd3-slab__glass-sheen {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background-image: linear-gradient(150deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0) 44%);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3), inset 0 0 16px rgba(40, 60, 70, 0.26);
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* ---- Highlights, all driven off one attribute -------------------- */

.fg-cd3-slab__hl {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 6;
  inset: 0;
  opacity: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
}

.fg-cd3-slab__hl--line { z-index: 10; }

.fg-cd3-slab__hl-clip {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  clip-path: var(--slab-skin-arc);
}

.fg-cd3-slab__hl i,
.fg-cd3-slab__hl-clip i { position: absolute; display: block; }

/* Skin: ringed and lit, masked by the arc and holed at the cassette. */
.fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='skin'] {
  clip-path: var(--slab-skin-hole);

  i {
    inset: 0;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px #2eac66, inset 0 0 30px -10px rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.9);
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 56% 78% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 99.5%, #000 100%);
    mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 56% 78% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 99.5%, #000 100%);
    -webkit-mask-size: cover;
    mask-size: cover;
  }
}

/* Foam: inside the inner arc only. */
.fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='foam'] i {
  inset: 7% 8.6%;
  background: rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.24);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.9);
  clip-path: var(--slab-board-arc);
}

/* Board: the band BETWEEN the arcs, which is the only place it is
   exposed. Inside the skin cut, outside the board cut. */
.fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='board'] { clip-path: var(--slab-skin-arc); }

.fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='board'] i {
  inset: 7% 8.6%;
  background: rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.22);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.9);
  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 36% 50% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 99.4%, #000 100%);
  mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 36% 50% at 100% 0, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 99.4%, #000 100%);
  -webkit-mask-size: cover;
  mask-size: cover;
}

/* Rails: filled where the cut exposes them, dashed where they continue
   under the skin, so you can see where the rail goes. */
.fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='poly'] .fg-cd3-slab__hl-clip i,
.fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='timber'] .fg-cd3-slab__hl-clip i {
  background: rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.1);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px #2eac66;
}

.fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='poly'] .fg-cd3-slab__hl-clip {
  .is-l { top: 0; bottom: 0; left: 0; width: 3.4%; }
  .is-r { top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; width: 3.4%; }
  .is-t { top: 0; right: 0; left: 0; height: 2.2%; }
  .is-b { right: 0; bottom: 0; left: 0; height: 2.2%; }
}

.fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='poly-line'] {
  .is-l { top: 0; bottom: 0; left: 0; width: 3.4%; border-right: 1px dashed rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.95); }
  .is-r { top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; width: 3.4%; border-left: 1px dashed rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.95); }
  .is-t { top: 0; right: 0; left: 0; height: 2.2%; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.95); }
}

.fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='timber'] .fg-cd3-slab__hl-clip {
  .is-ap { top: 13.882%; left: 17.4%; width: 47.2%; height: 20.236%; }
  .is-l { top: 0; bottom: 0; left: 3.4%; width: 5.2%; }
  .is-r { top: 0; right: 3.4%; bottom: 0; width: 5.2%; }
  .is-t { top: 2.2%; right: 3.4%; left: 3.4%; height: 4.4%; }
  .is-b { right: 3.4%; bottom: 2.2%; left: 3.4%; height: 4.6%; }
}

.fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='timber-line'] {
  .is-l,
  .is-r {
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 5.2%;
    border-right: 1px dashed rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.95);
    border-left: 1px dashed rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.95);
  }

  .is-l { left: 3.4%; }
  .is-r { right: 3.4%; }
}

/* ---- Callouts ---------------------------------------------------- */

.fg-cd3-slab__callouts {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 0.43;
  pointer-events: none;
}

.fg-cd3-slab__ring {
  position: absolute;
  top: 15%;
  left: 20%;
  width: 42%;
  height: 18%;
  outline: 1px dashed var(--slab-green);
  outline-offset: 3px;
  opacity: 0;
}

.fg-cd3-slab__dot {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 11;
  top: var(--dot-y, 41%);
  left: var(--dot-x, 20%);
  width: 9px;
  height: 9px;
  border: 2px solid var(--slab-green);
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: #ffffff;
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 4px rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.2);
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}

.fg-cd3-slab__chip {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 12;
  top: var(--dot-y, 41%);
  left: var(--dot-x, 20%);
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 6px;
  opacity: 0;

  > span {
    padding: 4px 11px;
    border: 1px solid #e1e7ea;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: #ffffff;
    color: #002d3a;
    font-size: 11.5px;
    font-weight: 600;
    white-space: nowrap;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 12px -6px rgba(0, 45, 58, 0.35);
  }

  /* The tick runs from the dot to the chip, so a chip always points back
     at the thing it is measuring. */
  &::before {
    content: '';
    width: 13px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--slab-green);
  }
}

.fg-cd3-slab__chip--right { transform: translate(0, -50%); }

/* Chips flip to the left of their dot past 62% across so none of them
   runs outside the figure. */
.fg-cd3-slab__chip--left {
  flex-direction: row-reverse;
  transform: translate(-100%, -50%);
}

/* ---- The one attribute that drives all of it --------------------- */

.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='0'] { --dot-x: 20%; --dot-y: 41%; }
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='1'] { --dot-x: 98.3%; --dot-y: 33%; }
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='2'] { --dot-x: 94%; --dot-y: 24%; }
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='3'] { --dot-x: 64%; --dot-y: 32%; }
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='4'] { --dot-x: 86%; --dot-y: 10%; }
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='5'] { --dot-x: 41%; --dot-y: 24%; }

.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='0'] .fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='skin'],
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='1'] .fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='poly'],
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='1'] .fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='poly-line'],
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='2'] .fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='timber'],
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='2'] .fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='timber-line'],
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='3'] .fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='board'],
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='4'] .fg-cd3-slab__hl[data-hl='foam'] { opacity: 1; }

.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='0'] .fg-cd3-slab__chip[data-chip='0'],
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='1'] .fg-cd3-slab__chip[data-chip='1'],
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='2'] .fg-cd3-slab__chip[data-chip='2'],
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='3'] .fg-cd3-slab__chip[data-chip='3'],
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='4'] .fg-cd3-slab__chip[data-chip='4'],
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='5'] .fg-cd3-slab__chip[data-chip='5'] { opacity: 1; }

/* The glass row lifts and rings the cassette rather than tinting it,
   because tinting glass green reads as a coloured pane. */
.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='5'] .fg-cd3-slab__cassette {
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px var(--slab-green), 0 14px 30px -10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
}

.fg-cd3-anatomy[data-active-layer='5'] .fg-cd3-slab__ring {
  outline-offset: 11px;
  opacity: 0.65;
}

/* None of the movement is load-bearing: the highlight, the dot and the
   chip are all correct at rest. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .fg-cd3-slab__hl { transition: opacity 0.35s ease; }
  .fg-cd3-slab__dot { transition: left 0.5s var(--slab-ease), top 0.5s var(--slab-ease); }
  .fg-cd3-slab__chip { transition: opacity 0.35s ease; }
  .fg-cd3-slab__ring { transition: opacity 0.35s ease, outline-offset 0.5s var(--slab-ease); }
}

/* ---- Chapter three: the guarantee -------------------------------- */

/* The badge is drawn in `currentColor` now, so it takes the panel's own
   ink instead of being a filled navy shield with a white band that read
   as clip art beside a real photograph. */
.fg-cd3-security .fg-cd3-shield {
  display: block;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);

  svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
}

/* The four supporting points were mid-grey on dark navy. */
.fg-cd3-security__points li span { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.76); }

/* ---- Chapter three: the handoff ---------------------------------- */

/* It sat alone with ~135px of dead canvas above it and a button
   floating at the far right. It is the chapter's footer now, so it sits
   tight under the guarantee and reads as the next sentence. */
.generated-page--composite-doors > .fg-wd-cta { padding-top: clamp(14px, 1.6vw, 22px); }

.fg-wd-cta__inner { gap: clamp(1.25rem, 2.4vw, 2.25rem); }

/* ---- Chapter four: the finishes ---------------------------------- */

.fg-finish {
  display: grid;
  gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
}

.fg-finish > section { padding-block: 0 !important; }

.fg-finish__step-head {
  max-width: 60ch;
  margin: 0 0 clamp(1.1rem, 2.2vw, 1.6rem);

  .eyebrow { margin: 0 0 0.45rem; }

  h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 1.9vw, 1.45rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
  }

  > p {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.72);
    font-size: 0.96rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  > p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
}

/* THE COLOUR SECTION NO LONGER PAINTS ITS OWN BACKGROUND. It carried a
   band that met the page canvas in a visible horizontal seam, which is
   the banding fault the Site-Wide Background Rule records against this
   exact page. */
.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-colour { background: transparent; }

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-colour__layout {
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 0.62fr);
  align-items: start;
}

.fg-cd3-colour__note { max-width: 72ch; }

/* Eleven designs in a five-column grid left one card alone beside four
   empty cells, and the image rows resolved to different heights inside
   the same row because each caption is a different length. A fixed
   proportion on the figure fixes the second; four columns leaves a last
   row of three rather than an orphan of one. */
.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-composite-glass__grid {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
}

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-composite-glass-card {
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr;
  min-height: 0;
}

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-composite-glass-card figure { aspect-ratio: 4 / 3; }

/* THE NUMBERED BADGES IMPLIED A RANKING THAT DOES NOT EXIST. The order
   in `site-data.php` is not a preference, and eleven of twenty-six
   designs is not a top eleven. */
.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-composite-glass-card__number { display: none; }

/* Three consecutive note blocks in three different treatments closed
   the section. One measure, one voice. */
.fg-composite-glass__aperture,
.fg-composite-glass__range,
.fg-composite-glass__note { max-width: 72ch; }

/* ---- Chapter five, and the tail ---------------------------------- */

/* The FAQ's left column held a heading and then ~425px of empty space
   for the accordion's full height. It follows the questions now. */
.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-product-faq__grid > *:first-child {
  position: sticky;
  top: 96px;
}

/* ---- Page rhythm, rewritten -------------------------------------- */

/* The old rule listed `.fg-cd3-brief` and `.fg-composite-approved`,
   both of which moved into the hero, and put the range and the quiz on
   separate surfaces. Five chapters need different joins: generous
   between chapters, tight inside one. */
.generated-page--composite-doors {
  > section { padding-block: clamp(52px, 6vw, 84px); }

  > .fg-cd3-hero { padding-block: 0; }

  > .fg-choose { padding-block: clamp(44px, 5vw, 72px); }

  /* The cutaway and the guarantee are one chapter, so they sit close
     and the handoff closes it. */
  > .fg-cd3-anatomy { padding-bottom: clamp(24px, 3vw, 40px); }
  > .fg-cd3-security { padding-top: clamp(24px, 3vw, 40px); }

  /* The choosing surface is supplied by `.fg-choose__surface`; the old
     rule put it on `.fg-cds > .container`, which no longer exists in
     that shape. */
  > .fg-cds > .container {
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
    background: none;
    box-shadow: none;
  }
}

/* ---- Mobile ------------------------------------------------------ */

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  /* THE DOOR MUST BE IN THE FIRST FOLD. It used to sit below both CTAs,
     so a phone opened on a wall of text and two buttons. */
  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero { min-height: 0; }

  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero__grid {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1.35rem;
  }

  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero__media { order: -1; }

  .fg-cd3-hero__media img {
    max-height: 46svh;
    object-position: center 38%;
  }

  .fg-cd3-hero__credential {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
    padding-top: 1rem;
  }

  .fg-cd3-hero__facts {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));

    > div + div { border-left: 0; }
    > div:nth-child(2n) { border-left: 1px solid rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.1); }
    > div:nth-child(n + 3) { border-top: 1px solid rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.1); }
  }

  .fg-choose__surface {
    padding: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 1.5rem);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .fg-cds__grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }

  .fg-cds-door__link img { max-height: 132px; }

  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-composite-glass__grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }

  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-colour__layout {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-product-faq__grid > *:first-child { position: static; }

  /* The figure crops harder so the cut corner stays in frame. */
  .fg-cd3-slab { top: -22%; }

  .fg-cd3-slab__chip > span {
    padding: 3px 9px;
    font-size: 10.5px;
  }
}

@media (max-width: 520px) {
  .fg-cds__grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
}

/* ==================================================================
 * `/why-distinction/`, 2026-08-27 overhaul
 *
 * Four sections, 4,689px, ZERO photographs, and about 700px of empty
 * viewport beside every paragraph at 1440. Both six-item grids ran four
 * across, so each left two cards next to a two-cell hole.
 *
 * It carries the same grammar as the composite page's construction
 * chapter now — same numerals, same card treatment, same accent — so a
 * reader arriving from the through-link recognises where they are.
 * ================================================================== */

.fg-wd-hero__grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 0.78fr);
  gap: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
  align-items: center;
}

.fg-wd-hero__media {
  margin: 0;

  img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 480px;
    border-radius: 10px;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 40%;
    box-shadow: 0 28px 64px -34px rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.55), 0 2px 6px rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.1);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.55);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
}

/* THE HANDOFF FROM THE DRAWING. The composite page owns the graphic and
   this page owns the argument; saying so is what stops the two reading
   as two accounts of the same slab. */
.fg-wd-slab__handoff {
  max-width: 62ch;
  margin: 1rem 0 0;
  padding-left: 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid var(--fg-green);
  color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.7);
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  line-height: 1.6;

  a {
    color: var(--fg-green);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
}

/* Six layers, three across. Four across left two cards beside a
   two-cell hole. */
.fg-wd-layers,
.fg-wd-grid {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
}

/* The card's contents anchor to its edges so a short one does not carry
   the row's spare height as an internal void. */
.fg-wd-layer,
.fg-wd-card {
  display: grid;
  align-content: start;
  gap: 0.45rem;
}

.fg-wd-layer h3,
.fg-wd-card h3 { margin: 0; }

.fg-wd-layer p,
.fg-wd-card p { margin: 0; }

/* ---- The figure -------------------------------------------------- */

.fg-wd-figure__grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.92fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3.4vw, 3rem);
  align-items: start;
  margin-top: clamp(0.5rem, 1.4vw, 1rem);
}

.fg-wd-figure__claim p {
  max-width: 42ch;
  line-height: 1.6;
}

.fg-wd-stat span {
  /* It was the largest green display type on the site, floating with
     nothing to hold it. Sized against the paragraph it belongs to. */
  font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 4.4vw, 3.4rem);
}

.fg-wd-figure__caveats {
  padding: clamp(1.1rem, 2.4vw, 1.75rem);
  border: 1px solid rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.12);
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7);

  h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.85rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.85rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.78);
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    top: 0.72em;
    left: 0;
    width: 6px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--fg-green);
  }
}

/* ---- The judgement ----------------------------------------------- */

.fg-wd-judgement__grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 0.82fr);
  gap: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 3.25rem);
  align-items: center;
}

.fg-wd-judgement__words p { max-width: 56ch; }

.fg-wd-judgement__media {
  margin: 0;

  img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 10px;
    object-fit: cover;
    box-shadow: 0 24px 56px -32px rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.5);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.55);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
  }
}

/* ---- Where to go next -------------------------------------------- */

/* It used to end with two buttons pointing at the top of
   `/composite-doors/`, which is not a place anybody wants to go back
   to. These name what is actually at the other end. */
.fg-wd-next__grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: clamp(0.85rem, 2vw, 1.35rem);
}

.fg-wd-next__card {
  display: grid;
  align-content: start;
  gap: 0.4rem;
  padding: clamp(1rem, 2.2vw, 1.4rem);
  border: 1px solid rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.12);
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: #ffffff;
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
  transition: border-color 160ms ease, box-shadow 160ms ease, transform 160ms ease;

  strong {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    color: var(--fg-ink, #022b35);
    font-size: 1.02rem;
  }

  strong::after {
    content: "\2192";
    color: var(--fg-green);
    transition: transform 160ms ease;
  }

  span {
    color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.7);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
}

.fg-wd-next__card:hover,
.fg-wd-next__card:focus-visible {
  border-color: rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.5);
  box-shadow: 0 16px 34px -20px rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.42);
  transform: translateY(-2px);

  strong::after { transform: translateX(3px); }
}

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .fg-wd-hero__grid,
  .fg-wd-judgement__grid,
  .fg-wd-figure__grid {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  /* The door before the argument, the same call as the composite hero. */
  .fg-wd-hero__media { order: -1; }

  .fg-wd-hero__media img { max-height: 44svh; }

  .fg-wd-layers,
  .fg-wd-grid,
  .fg-wd-next__grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }
}

@media (max-width: 560px) {
  .fg-wd-layers,
  .fg-wd-grid,
  .fg-wd-next__grid {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }
}

/* An answer that points at the page carrying it in full. Added with the
   optional `link` key on a FAQ entry, 2026-08-27. */
.fg-product-faq__more {
  margin: 0.7rem 0 0;

  a {
    color: var(--fg-green);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
}

/* CHAPTER OPENERS BEAT THE ROUTE'S BLANKET HEADING RULE. That rule is
   `.generated-page--composite-doors h2` at (0,2,1), so a bare
   `.fg-chapter-head h2` at (0,1,1) loses to it however far down the file it
   sits. Scoped to the route here rather than by raising the blanket rule's
   reach, so `/why-distinction/` keeps using the same class unscoped. */
.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-chapter-head h2,
.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cds__head h2,
.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-anatomy__head h2 {
  max-width: 24ch;
  font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 2.9vw, 2.35rem);
  line-height: 1.08;
  letter-spacing: -0.014em;
}

/* ---- Corrections after the first render on test ------------------ */

/* THE HERO MEDIA BOX IS SHARED WITH `/heritage-aluminium-doors/` AND IS
   `aspect-ratio: 6/5`, which is landscape. Dropping a portrait photograph into
   it cropped a whole door and sidelight down to a letterbox: soffit at the top,
   threshold gone at the bottom. The box is portrait on this route only, so the
   heritage hero is untouched. `STYLE.md`: a crop that cuts the product out of
   frame is a defect, not a detail. */
.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero__media {
  width: min(100%, 540px);
  aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: transparent;
  box-shadow: 0 30px 70px -34px rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.55), 0 2px 6px rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.1);
}

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero__media img {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  max-height: none;
  border-radius: inherit;
  object-fit: cover;
  object-position: center 46%;
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* The wordmark was rendering at 104px, which is 23px tall and reads as a
   smudge under a rule. */
.fg-cd3-hero__credential img { width: 132px; }

/* The drawing was getting under a third of the explorer, so a slab that is
   0.43 as wide as it is tall came out too small to read the layers in. */
.fg-cd3-anatomy__explorer {
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.82fr) minmax(0, 1.18fr);
}

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero__media {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 5 / 4;
  }

  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero__media img {
    object-position: center 40%;
  }
}

/* WHAT THE BLANKET `!important` WAS HIDING. With every h2 on the route forced
   to 28.8px, four sections' own oversized rules never showed: the quiz and the
   FAQ at 43.2px, the glass step at 38.88px, and the enquiry form at 57.6px,
   which is `--fg-font-size-max` exactly and is a page-hero size on a form
   heading. `STYLE.md` is explicit that enquiry headings are supporting content,
   not page heroes. Suppressing them all with one blanket rule is what removed
   the page's hierarchy in the first place; these are sized deliberately instead.

   NOTE FOR WHOEVER PICKS THIS UP: the oversized rules themselves are SITE-WIDE
   and still apply on every other route. `FULL-SITE-AUDIT-2026-08-13.md` already
   carries heading inconsistency as an open finding. This fixes one page, not
   the system. */
.generated-page--composite-doors {
  /* The three steps of the finishes chapter, matching each other and sitting
     under the chapter opener rather than competing with it. */
  .fg-composite-glass h2,
  .fg-handle-finishes__heading h2 {
    max-width: 60ch;
    font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 1.9vw, 1.45rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.006em;
  }

  /* Everything else on the page at the house h2 size. */
  .fg-cdq__head h2,
  .fg-product-faq h2,
  .fg-enquiry h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 2.2vw, 2rem);
    line-height: 1.12;
  }
}

/* The door cards were bottom-heavy: the drawing was capped at 190px while the
   grid row stretched taller, so the surplus fell out as a band of white under
   the label. The drawing takes it instead. */
.fg-cds-door__link {
  gap: 0.4rem;
  padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
}

.fg-cds-door__link img { max-height: 230px; }

/* The handoff is the construction chapter's footer, so it sits under the
   guarantee rather than 106px below it. */
.generated-page--composite-doors > .fg-cd3-security { padding-bottom: clamp(24px, 3vw, 40px); }

/* The long page's hero: the copy column is shorter than a portrait
   photograph, so `align-items: center` was leaving a band of canvas under both.
   Held to the media's own height instead. */
.fg-wd-hero__media img { max-height: 460px; }

.fg-wd-page > section:first-child { padding-bottom: clamp(30px, 3.6vw, 48px); }

/* THE MOBILE CAP HAD TO MOVE, and this is the ordinary trap of appending a
   corrections block: `.fg-cds-door__link img { max-height: 230px }` was written
   after the `@media (max-width: 860px)` rule that capped the same image at
   132px, and at equal specificity the later one wins at every width. A phone
   was rendering 344px door cards, so nineteen doors ran to 3,528px on their
   own. Re-declared here, after it. */
@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .fg-cds-door__link img { max-height: 128px; }

  .fg-cds-door__link {
    gap: 0.3rem;
    padding: 0.55rem 0.5rem 0.45rem;
  }

  .fg-cds-door__name { font-size: 0.74rem; }

  /* The hover cue is unreachable on touch and only costs height there. */
  .fg-cds-door__cue { display: none; }
}

/* ---- The finishes chapter on a phone -----------------------------
 * Eleven glass designs in two columns is six rows, and eight handles is
 * four more: 3,698px of the mobile page spent scrolling past swatches
 * one pair at a time. `STYLE.md` asks for native horizontal scroll-snap
 * on mobile rails, with an obvious affordance, and lists "clipped next
 * item" as one. The cards are 62vw, so the next one is always half in
 * frame and there is no invented control to learn.
 *
 * The desktop grids are untouched: this is a different layout for a
 * different screen, which is what "mobile is designed, not squeezed"
 * means.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-composite-glass__grid,
  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-handle-finishes__grid {
    display: flex;
    grid-template-columns: none;
    overflow-x: auto;
    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
    scroll-padding-left: clamp(0.75rem, 3vw, 1rem);
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
    -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
    scrollbar-width: none;
  }

  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-composite-glass__grid::-webkit-scrollbar,
  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-handle-finishes__grid::-webkit-scrollbar {
    display: none;
  }

  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-composite-glass__grid > *,
  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-handle-finishes__grid > * {
    flex: 0 0 62vw;
    max-width: 62vw;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
  }
}

/* THE GRID MIN-WIDTH TRAP, AND IT COST TWO BLANK PHONE VIEWPORTS.
   `.fg-finish` is a grid, and a grid track's minimum is `auto`, which
   resolves to the max-content width of its widest child. The colour
   wall's 27 swatches are an `auto-fit` grid with a minimum track size,
   so its max-content is over 1,100px, and that became the width of the
   whole chapter column: on a 390px phone the colour section laid itself
   out 1,180px wide and 1,809px tall, entirely off to the right of the
   screen. It read as two viewports of empty canvas, and it did not trip
   the horizontal-overflow check because an ancestor clipped it.
   `minmax(0, 1fr)` lets the track shrink below its content. */
.fg-finish { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

/* The layer list stretches to the drawing's height so the card has no dead
   corner, but with the drawing given a wider column the rows grew and each
   one's content stayed pinned to the top of its row, leaving a band of nothing
   under every heading. Centring the content in the row it was already given
   spends that space instead of showing it. */
.fg-cd3-layer {
  display: grid;
  align-content: center;
}

/* The long page's hero photograph is portrait and was being cropped to a
   landscape band by a plain `max-height`, which took the top off the door and
   the fitter holding it. Held at its own proportion, like the composite hero. */
.fg-wd-hero__media {
  width: min(100%, 480px);
  justify-self: end;

  img {
    max-height: none;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    object-position: center 42%;
  }
}

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .fg-wd-hero__media {
    width: 100%;
    justify-self: stretch;
  }

  .fg-wd-hero__media img { aspect-ratio: 5 / 4; }
}

/* The glass step's head is a two-column grid aligned to its baseline, which
   sat its intro paragraph above and to the right of its own heading. Inside the
   finishes chapter it is one of three steps and has to read like the other two,
   so it takes the same single-column head. */
.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-composite-glass__head {
  display: block;
  max-width: 60ch;
}

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-composite-glass__head > div { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }

/* THE LAST SEAM ON THE PAGE. The glass step paints its own gradient panel,
   which met the shared canvas in a visible horizontal line partway down the
   finishes chapter: the same banding fault the Site-Wide Background Rule
   records against this page, and the reason the colour step lost its band
   earlier in this block. The grid below already supplies local contrast. */
.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-composite-glass { background: transparent; }

/* ==================================================================
 * COMPOSITE DOORS, SECOND PASS, 2026-08-27
 *
 * The owner reviewed the first pass and rejected four things. Each is
 * answered here, and each was a real fault:
 *
 *   "the hero image is not a hero image, it shouldn't be in that little
 *    box"                       -> full bleed, and a studio render
 *   "the style range, they're absolutely massive, that's five different
 *    viewports"                 -> a contact sheet, not a card wall
 *   "this doesn't look like a BuzzFeed quiz at all"
 *                               -> measured one and took its grammar
 *   "the finishes, there's nothing there" / "random boxes of text
 *    everywhere"                -> an index that carries content, one note
 * ================================================================== */

/* ---- Chapter one: a hero that behaves like one --------------------
 * It was a portrait photograph in the boxed 6/5 media the heritage door
 * page uses, beside a column of copy. That is a product page pattern,
 * not a hero. This is full bleed with the copy on a scrim, which is the
 * one thing the composition was missing: a single dominant object.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero {
  position: relative;
  display: grid;
  align-content: end;
  gap: 0;
  min-height: min(860px, calc(100svh - 76px));
  padding-block: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  isolation: isolate;
}

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero__media {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: -1;
  inset: 0;
  width: auto;
  aspect-ratio: auto;
  margin: 0;
  border-radius: 0;
  background: var(--color-steel, #002d3a);
  box-shadow: none;

  img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    max-height: none;
    border-radius: 0;
    object-fit: cover;
    /* The door sits right of centre in the render, so the crop holds the
       right two thirds and the copy takes the wall on the left. */
    object-position: 62% 50%;
    box-shadow: none;
  }
}

/* Dark enough on the left for white type at AA, clear enough on the
   right that the door is still the thing you look at. */
.fg-cd3-hero__scrim {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background:
    linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(0, 24, 31, 0.93) 0%, rgba(0, 24, 31, 0.82) 34%, rgba(0, 24, 31, 0.34) 62%, rgba(0, 24, 31, 0.16) 100%),
    linear-gradient(0deg, rgba(0, 24, 31, 0.6) 0%, rgba(0, 24, 31, 0) 42%);
}

.fg-cd3-hero__inner {
  padding-top: clamp(4rem, 12vh, 8rem);
  padding-bottom: clamp(2rem, 5vh, 3.5rem);
}

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero__copy {
  max-width: 40rem;

  .eyebrow { color: #7fe0aa; }

  h1 {
    max-width: 13ch;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: #ffffff;
  }
}

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero__lead {
  max-width: 44ch;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86);
}

.fg-cd3-hero__credential {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 1rem;
  max-width: 46ch;
  margin: 1.6rem 0 0;
  padding-top: 1.35rem;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.22);

  img {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 132px;
    height: auto;
  }

  span {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.74);
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
}

/* The facts ride on the hero's own foot, so the fold is one composition
   rather than a hero and then a strip. */
.fg-cd3-hero__facts-band {
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
  background: rgba(0, 24, 31, 0.58);
  backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
}

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cd3-hero__facts {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
  margin: 0;
  overflow: visible;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 0;
  background: none;
  backdrop-filter: none;

  > div {
    padding: 1.05rem 1.35rem;
    border-left: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
  }

  > div:first-child { padding-left: 0; border-left: 0; }

  dt {
    margin: 0 0 0.22rem;
    color: #ffffff;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-weight: 750;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    font-size: 0.79rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }
}

/* ---- Chapter two: the range as a contact sheet -------------------
 * Nineteen doors in five columns at 230px a drawing was 2,200px, and
 * Esprit's forty-three ran to five viewports. The drawings are simple
 * outlines and they read at a tenth of that size, so this is a contact
 * sheet: dense, scannable, one collection on a screen or two.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

.fg-cds__grid {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(96px, 1fr));
  gap: 8px;
}

.fg-cds-door__link {
  grid-template-rows: auto auto;
  gap: 0.3rem;
  padding: 0.5rem 0.4rem 0.45rem;
  border-color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.08);
  border-radius: 5px;
  background: rgba(247, 249, 248, 0.9);
}

.fg-cds-door__link img {
  max-height: 124px;
  opacity: 0.72;
}

.fg-cds-door__name {
  min-height: 2.4em;
  font-size: 0.66rem;
  line-height: 1.2;
  letter-spacing: -0.005em;
}

/* At this size the cue is a fifth of the tile, so it becomes the hover
   state of the whole card instead of a permanent row. */
.fg-cds-door__cue { display: none; }

.fg-cds-door__link:hover,
.fg-cds-door__link:focus-visible {
  border-color: rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.55);
  background: #ffffff;
  box-shadow: 0 10px 22px -14px rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.45);
  transform: translateY(-2px);

  img { opacity: 1; }
  .fg-cds-door__name { color: var(--fg-green); }
}

/* ---- Chapter two: the quiz, as a quiz ----------------------------
 * Measured against a real BuzzFeed quiz: questions at 80px/800 and
 * centred, every answer a flat block with square corners, picture
 * first. The grammar transfers; the hot pink does not. The saturation
 * here is the page's second dark band, and the type does the shouting.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

.fg-cdq {
  padding: clamp(3.5rem, 8vw, 6rem) 0 clamp(3.5rem, 8vw, 5.5rem);
  background:
    radial-gradient(120% 80% at 82% 0%, rgba(46, 172, 102, 0.22), transparent 62%),
    var(--color-steel, #002d3a);
  color: #ffffff;
}

.generated-page--composite-doors > .fg-cdq { padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 8vw, 6rem) clamp(3.5rem, 8vw, 5.5rem); }

.fg-cdq__head {
  max-width: 46rem;
  margin: 0 auto clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
  text-align: center;
}

.fg-cdq__badge {
  display: inline-block;
  margin: 0 0 1rem;
  padding: 0.34rem 0.85rem;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: var(--fg-green, #2eac66);
  color: #04231a;
  font-size: 0.7rem;
  font-weight: 800;
  letter-spacing: 0.16em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}

.generated-page--composite-doors .fg-cdq__head h2 {
  max-width: none;
  margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  color: #ffffff;
  font-size: clamp(2rem, 5.4vw, var(--fg-font-size-max, 3.6rem));
  font-weight: 800;
  line-height: 0.98;
  letter-spacing: -0.028em;
}

.fg-cdq__standfirst {
  max-width: 54ch;
  margin: 0 auto;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.74);
  font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.3vw, 1.1rem);
  line-height: 1.55;
}

.fg-cdq__panel {
  max-width: 62rem;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.fg-cdq__panel[hidden] { display: none !important; }

/* ---- progress ---- */

.fg-cdq__progress {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 1.1rem;
  margin: 0 0 clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 2.75rem);
}

.fg-cdq__count {
  margin: 0;
  color: #ffffff;
  font-size: 1.35rem;
  font-weight: 800;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  white-space: nowrap;
}

.fg-cdq__bar {
  display: grid;
  grid-auto-flow: column;
  grid-auto-columns: 1fr;
  gap: 5px;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
}

.fg-cdq__pip {
  height: 4px;
  border-radius: 2px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
  transition: background 260ms ease;
}

.fg-cdq__pip.is-done { background: var(--fg-green, #2eac66); }

/* ---- the question ---- */

.fg-cdq__q { text-align: center; }
.fg-cdq__q[hidden] { display: none !important; }

.fg-cdq__kicker {
  margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  color: #7fe0aa;
  font-size: 0.72rem;
  font-weight: 800;
  letter-spacing: 0.16em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}

.fg-cdq__title {
  max-width: 20ch;
  margin: 0 auto clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
  color: #ffffff;
  font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4.2vw, 2.9rem);
  font-weight: 800;
  line-height: 1.02;
  letter-spacing: -0.026em;
  text-wrap: balance;
}

/* ---- the answers ---- */

.fg-cdq__answers {
  display: grid;
  gap: clamp(0.7rem, 1.6vw, 1.1rem);
  margin: 0;
  text-align: left;
}

.fg-cdq__answers--2 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
.fg-cdq__answers--3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
.fg-cdq__answers--4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }

.fg-cdq__answers--swatches {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
}

/* Square corners and a flat block, which is the whole of BuzzFeed's
   answer treatment and the reason theirs reads as a game and a bordered
   white box reads as a form. */
.fg-cdq__answer {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-rows: 1fr auto;
  gap: 0;
  padding: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 4px;
  background: #ffffff;
  color: var(--fg-ink, #022b35);
  text-align: left;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: transform 160ms ease, box-shadow 160ms ease;
}

.fg-cdq__answer-block {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-height: 0;
  padding: clamp(0.9rem, 2vw, 1.4rem);
  background: linear-gradient(170deg, #eef3f0, #dfe9e4);

  img {
    display: block;
    width: auto;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: clamp(112px, 15vw, 168px);
    object-fit: contain;
    opacity: 0.8;
  }
}

.fg-cdq__answer-text {
  display: block;
  padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem 0.85rem;

  strong {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    font-weight: 750;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  small {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.2rem;
    color: rgba(2, 43, 53, 0.62);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }
}

.fg-cdq__answer:hover,
.fg-cdq__answer:focus-visible {
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px var(--fg-green, #2eac66), 0 18px 34px -20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6);
  transform: translateY(-3px);

  .fg-cdq__answer-block img { opacity: 1; }
}

/* The colour step. Eight chips: the colour IS the answer, so it fills
   the block rather than sitting on a door that would compete with it. */
.fg-cdq__answer--swatch .fg-cdq__answer-block { padding: 0; }

.fg-cdq__chip {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 5 / 4;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 0;
}

/* ---- controls ---- */

.fg-cdq__controls {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  gap: 1.25rem;
  margin-top: clamp(1.1rem, 2.4vw, 1.75rem);
}

.fg-cdq__back,
.fg-cdq__skip-btn {
  padding: 0.45rem 0.2rem;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 0;
  background: transparent;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
  font: inherit;
  font-size: 0.9rem;
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-underline-offset: 4px;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: color 150ms ease;
}

.fg-cdq__back[hidden] { display: none !important; }

.fg-cdq__back:hover,
.fg-cdq__back:focus-visible,
.fg-cdq__skip-btn:hover,
.fg-cdq__skip-btn:focus-visible {
  background: transparent;
  color: #ffffff;
}

/* ---- the reveal ---- */

.fg-cdq__result[hidden] { display: none !important; }

.fg-cdq__reveal {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.68fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 3rem);
  align-items: center;
}

.fg-cdq__reveal-art {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
  border-radius: 6px;
  background: linear-gradient(170deg, #f4f8f6, #e2ece7);

  img {
    display: block;
    width: auto;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: clamp(240px, 34vw, 360px);
    object-fit: contain;
    opacity: 0.86;
  }
}

.fg-cdq__reveal-kicker {
  margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
  color: #7fe0aa;
  font-size: 0.74rem;
  font-weight: 800;
  letter-spacing: 0.18em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}

.fg-cdq__door-name {
  margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  color: #ffffff;
  font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.3rem);
  font-weight: 800;
  line-height: 0.98;
  letter-spacing: -0.03em;
}

