/* ============================================================================
   `/composite-doors/`: SECTIONS THAT ACTUALLY LAND, 2026-09-03
   Owner: "they arnt in one viewport they overlap. and you made other stuff
   look shit."

   BOTH TRUE, AND THE FIRST CAUSED THE SECOND.

   I had been proving "one viewport" by measuring section HEIGHT against the
   budget. Every section passed. But heights ranged 202px to 791px against a
   791px screen, so the slack ranged 0 to 589 - nothing aligned to anything,
   and every scroll stop showed the bottom of one section and the top of the
   next. Height under budget was never going to produce one-section-per-screen.
   Only alignment does.

   And because I was chasing a number, the cuts went wherever they were
   cheapest rather than wherever they were right: the slab drawing - the whole
   point of its section - was capped to 352px and left sitting in a void, and
   the case study photographs were cropped to 2:1, which beheads a door.

   Every one of those cuts is reverted here. The constraint is met by aligning
   sections to the viewport instead of shaving content out of them.
   ========================================================================= */

/* ---- Undo the damage ---------------------------------------------- */

.generated-page--composite-doors {

  /* The drawing is the section. It was capped at 352px inside a media box
     whose own ratio is 341/480, so it shrank away from its column and left
     113px of white under it. */
  .fg-cd3-anatomy__media { max-height: none; }

  /* 16/10 is what a house and a door both survive. 2:1 cut the tops off two
     of the three photographs. */
  .fg-cs-card__media { aspect-ratio: 16 / 10; }
  .fg-cs-strip__grid img { max-height: none; }

  .fg-glass-patterns img { max-height: none; }

  /* `rows="4"` is the designed height of that field. */
  .fg-enquiry .fg-form textarea { height: auto; min-height: 96px; }

  /* Type that was shrunk to buy pixels, back to the scale it was drawn at. */
  .fg-cd3-anatomy__lede { font-size: 1.02rem; }
  .fg-cdq__standfirst { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .fg-cs-card__summary { font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.55; }
}

/* ---- One section, one screen -------------------------------------- */

/* Desktop and a tall enough window only. On a phone a forced screen per
   section is fifteen screens of scrolling for content that reads fine in
   flow, and `100svh` there is a moving target as the browser chrome hides. */
@media (min-width: 1024px) and (min-height: 700px) {

  /* `proximity`, not `mandatory`. Mandatory traps a reader on any section
     that ever grows past the viewport - a longer FAQ answer, a wrapped
     heading at an odd width - with no way to reach the rest of it. Proximity
     settles the scroll onto a section boundary when it is near one, which is
     the behaviour that was actually missing, and costs nothing when it is
     not. */
  html:has(.generated-page--composite-doors) { scroll-snap-type: y proximity; }

  .generated-page--composite-doors > section,
  .generated-page--composite-doors .fg-finish > section {
    min-height: calc(100svh - 73px);
    scroll-snap-align: start;
    /* The header is sticky and 73px, so a section aligned to the top of the
       scrollport would start underneath it. */
    scroll-margin-top: 73px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  /* The hero sizes itself and paints its own full-bleed media; centring its
     children would fight the absolute positioning it already uses. */
  .generated-page--composite-doors > .fg-cd3-hero {
    display: grid;
    min-height: calc(100svh - 73px);
  }
}

/* ---- Screens that earn themselves ---------------------------------
 * Giving EVERY section a screen was the wrong reading of the rule. Two of
 * them were connective tissue, and a full viewport each turned them into
 * padding: the chapter head was a heading and three lines stranded in 600px
